1
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:05,540
All right.

2
00:00:05,540 --> 00:00:06,700
Great.
So hi, everybody.

3
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:10,560
Welcome to the second
lecture of CS25 this quarter.

4
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:16,720
And so today we're very lucky to
have two speakers here with us.

5
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:21,600
So Hazel or Heejeong Nam
is here in person today.

6
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:23,480
She's a master's student
at Brown University,

7
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:25,960
working on representation
learning, causality,

8
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:28,600
and self-supervised learning.

9
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:32,400
And we also have Lucas
Maes over Zoom, who

10
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:34,400
will be speaking afterwards.

11
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:37,280
And he's a PhD student at Mila
and the University of Montreal,

12
00:00:37,280 --> 00:00:39,840
working on JEPA and planning.

13
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:42,880
So I'm sure they're going to
give us very insightful talk

14
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:43,520
today.

15
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:48,120
And so without further ado,
I'll hand it off to Hazel.

16
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:48,780
Yeah.

17
00:00:48,780 --> 00:00:50,200
Thank you for introducing.

18
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:52,940
Hazel, first year master's
student in Brown University,

19
00:00:52,940 --> 00:00:55,520
working with Professor
Randall Balestriero.

20
00:00:55,520 --> 00:01:00,170
Our lab is working on JEPA,
self-supervised learning,

21
00:01:00,170 --> 00:01:01,590
and some theory as well.

22
00:01:04,410 --> 00:01:05,269
I'm a bit nervous.

23
00:01:05,269 --> 00:01:07,230
This is my first time
giving a talk in English,

24
00:01:07,230 --> 00:01:08,970
but at the same time,
I'm really, really

25
00:01:08,970 --> 00:01:11,770
excited to talk about JEPA
world model and my recent work,

26
00:01:11,770 --> 00:01:12,750
causal world model.

27
00:01:12,750 --> 00:01:14,530
I'm pretty sure Lucas
is also very excited

28
00:01:14,530 --> 00:01:19,730
to share his very recent
work, LeWorldModel.

29
00:01:19,730 --> 00:01:20,530
OK.

30
00:01:20,530 --> 00:01:23,290
So the first part
of this talk will

31
00:01:23,290 --> 00:01:26,930
be about the concepts
of JEPA and world model.

32
00:01:26,930 --> 00:01:30,170
And after a brief introduction
of those two concepts,

33
00:01:30,170 --> 00:01:33,330
we will talk about the
Causal-JEPA paper first,

34
00:01:33,330 --> 00:01:36,770
which gives a question about
how to make a model understand

35
00:01:36,770 --> 00:01:38,410
the object interaction.

36
00:01:38,410 --> 00:01:40,530
And then I will
hand over to Lucas,

37
00:01:40,530 --> 00:01:42,990
and he will talk
about LeWorldModel.

38
00:01:42,990 --> 00:01:46,770
And LeWorldModel is about
the end-to-end JEPA training

39
00:01:46,770 --> 00:01:49,930
without collapse.

40
00:01:49,930 --> 00:01:52,005
So first of all, I
think most of you guys

41
00:01:52,005 --> 00:01:54,130
may have heard of world
model, and some of you guys

42
00:01:54,130 --> 00:01:57,900
may be really familiar with
what is the world model.

43
00:01:57,900 --> 00:02:00,420
To talk about the world model--

44
00:02:00,420 --> 00:02:02,360
we should have this first.

45
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:04,460
So this is autoregressive model.

46
00:02:04,460 --> 00:02:08,220
You get the previous state,
and you predict the next step.

47
00:02:08,220 --> 00:02:11,560
However, this is sometimes not
enough to describe the world.

48
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:12,060
Why?

49
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:14,380
Because our world
has uncertainty.

50
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:16,740
At that time that
you're observing,

51
00:02:16,740 --> 00:02:19,500
someone might do
something at that time.

52
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:21,160
Someone can throw something.

53
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:22,820
There's inherent uncertainty.

54
00:02:22,820 --> 00:02:26,300
To handle this,
you need an action.

55
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:29,060
And this now is a world model.

56
00:02:29,060 --> 00:02:31,660
So the world model
basically is a function

57
00:02:31,660 --> 00:02:34,380
that gets the previous
state and the action

58
00:02:34,380 --> 00:02:36,900
to predict the next state.

59
00:02:36,900 --> 00:02:40,260
And in this sense, I perceive
the world model terminology

60
00:02:40,260 --> 00:02:42,140
as a simulator.

61
00:02:42,140 --> 00:02:44,980
So as an AI researcher,
what would you

62
00:02:44,980 --> 00:02:47,460
do to make this
world model better?

63
00:02:47,460 --> 00:02:52,300
In my opinion, I think
three points of designing.

64
00:02:52,300 --> 00:02:55,500
The first one is having a
good state representation.

65
00:02:55,500 --> 00:02:58,300
This is about-- so
you probably are not

66
00:02:58,300 --> 00:03:01,900
going to give the raw pixel
images or raw pixel values

67
00:03:01,900 --> 00:03:03,140
to the predictor.

68
00:03:03,140 --> 00:03:06,700
You somehow have to have
a good representation that

69
00:03:06,700 --> 00:03:09,940
reflects the world faithfully.

70
00:03:09,940 --> 00:03:13,140
And the second component will
be a good transition model.

71
00:03:13,140 --> 00:03:17,820
For example, there should
be a grounding rule

72
00:03:17,820 --> 00:03:19,680
underlying the environment.

73
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:23,860
For example, there's
a gravity that exists.

74
00:03:23,860 --> 00:03:26,920
We have to understand these
rules to predict the world

75
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:29,980
and to simulate the world.

76
00:03:29,980 --> 00:03:32,560
And the last component is
the good dynamics model.

77
00:03:32,560 --> 00:03:34,940
Now you're going
to do some action.

78
00:03:34,940 --> 00:03:37,940
And your model should
react to your action

79
00:03:37,940 --> 00:03:39,780
in the appropriate way.

80
00:03:39,780 --> 00:03:41,660
Keep these three things
in mind because I'll

81
00:03:41,660 --> 00:03:46,540
revisit this, end of
the Causal-JEPA part,

82
00:03:46,540 --> 00:03:50,900
to discuss how I address
these three questions.

83
00:03:50,900 --> 00:03:54,020
Today, we will not cover
generative world models.

84
00:03:54,020 --> 00:03:58,270
There's so many industries now
are doing the world modeling

85
00:03:58,270 --> 00:03:58,910
stuff.

86
00:03:58,910 --> 00:04:01,910
For example, GAIA is an
autonomous driving model

87
00:04:01,910 --> 00:04:03,750
that gets the action
in the previous state

88
00:04:03,750 --> 00:04:08,150
to predict the next
driving scenario.

89
00:04:08,150 --> 00:04:11,410
And the Genie, they want to
scaling up the world model,

90
00:04:11,410 --> 00:04:13,870
so they learned something
called latent action, which

91
00:04:13,870 --> 00:04:16,829
is a proxy of the
action, because usually

92
00:04:16,829 --> 00:04:20,950
online data doesn't have the
per frame action annotation.

93
00:04:20,950 --> 00:04:24,090
And Sora is a technically
video generation model.

94
00:04:24,090 --> 00:04:27,130
But their fidelity of their
generated scene is very good,

95
00:04:27,130 --> 00:04:29,510
so they got a lot of notice.

96
00:04:29,510 --> 00:04:32,510
And Marble from Professor
Fei-Fei Li and Stanford.

97
00:04:32,510 --> 00:04:38,790
They are doing the 3D
interactive environment so that

98
00:04:38,790 --> 00:04:42,030
we can interact or explore.

99
00:04:42,030 --> 00:04:44,550
But today, we will talk about
Joint-Embedding Predictive

100
00:04:44,550 --> 00:04:47,410
Architecture, which is somewhat
different from the generative

101
00:04:47,410 --> 00:04:49,070
world model.

102
00:04:49,070 --> 00:04:51,710
So on the left side,
the thing you see

103
00:04:51,710 --> 00:04:56,200
is generative world model,
because as you can see,

104
00:04:56,200 --> 00:04:57,780
the x is the current state.

105
00:04:57,780 --> 00:05:01,020
And let's say the y is the
thing that we have to predict,

106
00:05:01,020 --> 00:05:04,120
which is like a future state.

107
00:05:04,120 --> 00:05:07,880
You put the encoded current
set to the predictor,

108
00:05:07,880 --> 00:05:09,840
and you directly
compare to y, which

109
00:05:09,840 --> 00:05:15,880
means that your model should
give the same, the pixel space,

110
00:05:15,880 --> 00:05:17,840
as your target is.

111
00:05:17,840 --> 00:05:21,080
However, in the right side,
that is a Joint Predictive

112
00:05:21,080 --> 00:05:22,060
Architecture.

113
00:05:22,060 --> 00:05:25,520
You see both x and y are
going to the encoder.

114
00:05:25,520 --> 00:05:30,800
So now we are comparing our
prediction and the true target

115
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:32,560
in the latent space.

116
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:36,840
So why does it matter?

117
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:39,880
We don't have decoder in
the JEPA architecture.

118
00:05:39,880 --> 00:05:44,000
But it is not merely
about having no decoder.

119
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:47,560
It is about uncertainty
in the world.

120
00:05:47,560 --> 00:05:52,590
So when human is thinking about
what is going to happen next,

121
00:05:52,590 --> 00:05:58,130
are you predicting something
in a very pixel level,

122
00:05:58,130 --> 00:06:01,350
there's some kind of uncertainty
that you cannot predict.

123
00:06:01,350 --> 00:06:05,130
And there's obviously
like your interest in--

124
00:06:05,130 --> 00:06:07,290
your area of interest.

125
00:06:07,290 --> 00:06:13,650
So JEPA tries to deal with only
having predictive information

126
00:06:13,650 --> 00:06:17,450
in your latent space so that
your prediction is getting more

127
00:06:17,450 --> 00:06:20,650
meaningful and human-like way.

128
00:06:20,650 --> 00:06:24,138
And also, the framework can be
interpreted in a different way.

129
00:06:24,138 --> 00:06:25,930
First of all, the
generative world modeling

130
00:06:25,930 --> 00:06:27,450
is about a likelihood.

131
00:06:27,450 --> 00:06:29,170
They learn the
normalized likelihood

132
00:06:29,170 --> 00:06:30,470
over the future frames.

133
00:06:30,470 --> 00:06:34,490
But JEPA can be understood
as an energy-based model.

134
00:06:34,490 --> 00:06:38,010
So in the very original paper
that Yann LeCun suggested,

135
00:06:38,010 --> 00:06:42,770
he framed the JEPA as
an energy-based model.

136
00:06:42,770 --> 00:06:47,370
And energy-based model learns
the energy score function

137
00:06:47,370 --> 00:06:52,140
that gives the high value if two
values x and y are incompatible.

138
00:06:52,140 --> 00:06:55,120
And they give low score
if those are compatible.

139
00:06:55,120 --> 00:07:00,900
And compatibility in the world
model makes the point of the y

140
00:07:00,900 --> 00:07:04,680
is a plausible future of x

141
00:07:04,680 --> 00:07:09,280
So the energy-based model
can collapse, unfortunately.

142
00:07:09,280 --> 00:07:11,620
And there's two ways to
prevent this collapse.

143
00:07:11,620 --> 00:07:13,800
The first way is
contrastive learning.

144
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:17,740
And the second way is
regularization-based method.

145
00:07:17,740 --> 00:07:20,020
I'm not going to talk
about this too deeply,

146
00:07:20,020 --> 00:07:22,540
but the
regularization-based method

147
00:07:22,540 --> 00:07:27,260
are making very rich and
well-defined energy landscape.

148
00:07:27,260 --> 00:07:29,000
So that is what JEPA is doing.

149
00:07:32,220 --> 00:07:36,460
For example, V-JEPA,
which is Video-JEPA,

150
00:07:36,460 --> 00:07:41,740
they took on an input as
the consecutive frames

151
00:07:41,740 --> 00:07:43,900
with the spatiotemporal masking.

152
00:07:43,900 --> 00:07:46,380
And they put this encoder, and
they get the representation.

153
00:07:46,380 --> 00:07:51,160
And they try to predict what is
happening in the unseen area.

154
00:07:51,160 --> 00:07:53,780
So the target has
every information.

155
00:07:53,780 --> 00:07:59,100
And here, there's some
regularization tool

156
00:07:59,100 --> 00:08:00,120
to prevent collapse.

157
00:08:00,120 --> 00:08:01,960
For example, they
use EMA encoder.

158
00:08:01,960 --> 00:08:04,840
EMA here means Exponential
Moving Average.

159
00:08:04,840 --> 00:08:07,240
This prevents like
really trivial collapses.

160
00:08:07,240 --> 00:08:10,780
And also, you do stop gradient
for the target encoder,

161
00:08:10,780 --> 00:08:16,924
and you put the mask to give
a model more challenging task.

162
00:08:16,924 --> 00:08:20,720
V-JEPA 2 is basically the
same architecture as V-JEPA 1,

163
00:08:20,720 --> 00:08:22,980
but they wanted to scale up.

164
00:08:22,980 --> 00:08:26,700
And they also did some
interesting post-training.

165
00:08:26,700 --> 00:08:30,980
And one of them is
action-conditioned control.

166
00:08:30,980 --> 00:08:33,262
So now this became
more like word model

167
00:08:33,262 --> 00:08:35,720
because now it's getting-- as
you can see in the predictor,

168
00:08:35,720 --> 00:08:39,020
it gets the robot
action and poses.

169
00:08:39,020 --> 00:08:46,550
So this is one of the
post-training in the V-JEPA 2.

170
00:08:46,550 --> 00:08:49,390
And this is actually
called DINO World Model

171
00:08:49,390 --> 00:08:51,710
and have a very
same architecture

172
00:08:51,710 --> 00:08:56,310
as the action-conditioned
post-training that you just saw.

173
00:08:56,310 --> 00:08:59,030
So what the DINO
World Model is doing

174
00:08:59,030 --> 00:09:03,510
is they use the
frozen DINOv2 encoder.

175
00:09:03,510 --> 00:09:05,550
What they claim is,
oh, do we actually

176
00:09:05,550 --> 00:09:10,110
have to train the
JEPA encoder to get

177
00:09:10,110 --> 00:09:13,990
the meaningful
abstraction for planning?

178
00:09:13,990 --> 00:09:16,150
They said, no, a
pretrained DINO encoder

179
00:09:16,150 --> 00:09:19,950
can do that role as well.

180
00:09:19,950 --> 00:09:23,030
So they generate
patch representation.

181
00:09:23,030 --> 00:09:25,270
And with the auxiliary
variable, for example, action

182
00:09:25,270 --> 00:09:28,470
and proprioceptive signals,
they predict the future state

183
00:09:28,470 --> 00:09:32,950
representation,
and they compare.

184
00:09:32,950 --> 00:09:37,030
And this predictor is just
a simple Causal Transformer,

185
00:09:37,030 --> 00:09:40,270
which predicts the
future autoregressively.

186
00:09:40,270 --> 00:09:43,990
But here I want
you to think about,

187
00:09:43,990 --> 00:09:49,040
is the past representation
is what really humans do?

188
00:09:49,040 --> 00:09:54,400
Are we patchifying the image
to predict the next step?

189
00:09:54,400 --> 00:09:57,000
Probably not.

190
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,760
So today, we're going to
talk about Causal-JEPA,

191
00:09:59,760 --> 00:10:02,280
Learning World Models
through Object-Centric Latent

192
00:10:02,280 --> 00:10:05,680
Interventions.

193
00:10:05,680 --> 00:10:07,320
Before starting
this talk, I would

194
00:10:07,320 --> 00:10:11,320
like to thank you for my
collaborators, Quentin, Lucas,

195
00:10:11,320 --> 00:10:13,300
and Yann LeCun, and
Randall Balestriero.

196
00:10:15,920 --> 00:10:17,960
So the goal of
the Causal-JEPA is

197
00:10:17,960 --> 00:10:22,560
understanding the object
interaction and object dynamics.

198
00:10:22,560 --> 00:10:27,720
These are three data sets
that I picked to show today.

199
00:10:27,720 --> 00:10:31,200
The first one is Push-T,
a pretty famous example

200
00:10:31,200 --> 00:10:33,240
in the control experiments.

201
00:10:33,240 --> 00:10:37,040
The goal is you do
action on the blue ball,

202
00:10:37,040 --> 00:10:40,760
and you want to move the
gray T block to perfectly

203
00:10:40,760 --> 00:10:44,050
overlap on the green T.

204
00:10:44,050 --> 00:10:46,610
And the second data
set is CLEVRER.

205
00:10:46,610 --> 00:10:49,010
This is actually the
video paired with question

206
00:10:49,010 --> 00:10:51,610
answering the data set.

207
00:10:51,610 --> 00:10:52,610
The question is like--

208
00:10:52,610 --> 00:10:54,770
it can be predictory.

209
00:10:54,770 --> 00:10:57,390
For example, what would
happen in the next frame?

210
00:10:57,390 --> 00:10:58,790
And it can be counterfactual.

211
00:10:58,790 --> 00:11:03,290
For example, what would
happen if the blue color

212
00:11:03,290 --> 00:11:05,050
cylinder doesn't exist?

213
00:11:05,050 --> 00:11:06,910
And it can be explanatory.

214
00:11:06,910 --> 00:11:10,370
So it's basically VQA benchmark.

215
00:11:10,370 --> 00:11:11,890
And the third one is PHYRE.

216
00:11:11,890 --> 00:11:15,090
As you can see, many objects
are interacting each other

217
00:11:15,090 --> 00:11:16,190
with the grounding rule.

218
00:11:16,190 --> 00:11:19,130
For example, there's a gravity.

219
00:11:19,130 --> 00:11:22,390
The mass matters
here and et cetera.

220
00:11:27,490 --> 00:11:30,170
So to understand
this object dynamics,

221
00:11:30,170 --> 00:11:33,990
what the current models
are doing is like this.

222
00:11:33,990 --> 00:11:36,210
They patchify the
image, and they

223
00:11:36,210 --> 00:11:40,410
try to predict what will
happen in each patches.

224
00:11:40,410 --> 00:11:43,940
But what are you going
to do to understand

225
00:11:43,940 --> 00:11:49,260
this mechanism is you want to
understand the things like this.

226
00:11:49,260 --> 00:11:51,450
You have each
object and you want

227
00:11:51,450 --> 00:11:56,040
to know how one object
influences each other.

228
00:11:59,020 --> 00:12:03,180
So what if in the DINO World
Model, what if you just

229
00:12:03,180 --> 00:12:06,980
change the representation in the
object-centric representation?

230
00:12:06,980 --> 00:12:10,140
Then it's more close to
human-like thinking, right?

231
00:12:10,140 --> 00:12:13,680
But then we have to learn the
object-centric representation.

232
00:12:16,580 --> 00:12:20,420
I'm not going to do
very deeply about

233
00:12:20,420 --> 00:12:24,000
the object-centric learning,
but let me go through briefly.

234
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:25,500
If you're doing
computer vision, you

235
00:12:25,500 --> 00:12:28,620
might have heard about the
object-centric learning.

236
00:12:28,620 --> 00:12:32,420
There's a very foundational
way called slot attention

237
00:12:32,420 --> 00:12:34,740
from Locatello.

238
00:12:34,740 --> 00:12:38,220
This is basically
encoder-decoder framework.

239
00:12:38,220 --> 00:12:42,890
And between this
encoder and decoder,

240
00:12:42,890 --> 00:12:45,110
you might have the
feature space, right?

241
00:12:45,110 --> 00:12:48,390
And you bring like a
bucket, like a basket,

242
00:12:48,390 --> 00:12:50,810
to put each features.

243
00:12:50,810 --> 00:12:53,550
And this basket is
for each object.

244
00:12:53,550 --> 00:12:56,430
So there's a mechanism
called slot attention that

245
00:12:56,430 --> 00:13:00,570
puts each feature to each slot.

246
00:13:00,570 --> 00:13:03,430
So there's a binding
problem of feature to slot.

247
00:13:03,430 --> 00:13:05,910
And they decode
with this basket so

248
00:13:05,910 --> 00:13:10,350
that the model can have
well-aligned information

249
00:13:10,350 --> 00:13:11,370
with the slots.

250
00:13:14,265 --> 00:13:16,390
You don't have to read this
all, but I brought this

251
00:13:16,390 --> 00:13:19,550
because this is United
Transformers class.

252
00:13:19,550 --> 00:13:22,630
And slot attention is
about self-attention.

253
00:13:22,630 --> 00:13:25,190
So what I want to show is--

254
00:13:25,190 --> 00:13:28,350
how to bind the
features to the slot

255
00:13:28,350 --> 00:13:32,090
is you do self-attention, where
key is the input features.

256
00:13:32,090 --> 00:13:37,070
For example, in the DINOv2,
there's a patch embeddings.

257
00:13:37,070 --> 00:13:40,970
You allocate each patch
embedding to slots.

258
00:13:40,970 --> 00:13:42,950
You're basically assigning them.

259
00:13:42,950 --> 00:13:46,030
And the value is also
the input features.

260
00:13:46,030 --> 00:13:49,770
And you update the
slot by using GRU.

261
00:13:49,770 --> 00:13:52,450
So you iteratively
update the slots.

262
00:13:52,450 --> 00:13:55,930
And now the slot will have the
object-aligned representation.

263
00:13:55,930 --> 00:13:59,230
So this is how usually
the models are getting

264
00:13:59,230 --> 00:14:00,930
object-centric representation.

265
00:14:00,930 --> 00:14:05,670
This is very, very
naive approaches.

266
00:14:05,670 --> 00:14:07,210
There's so many advanced ways.

267
00:14:07,210 --> 00:14:10,030
For example, this is for
image, and there's a video slot

268
00:14:10,030 --> 00:14:11,550
attention models as well.

269
00:14:11,550 --> 00:14:15,390
So yeah, you can give it a try.

270
00:14:15,390 --> 00:14:20,350
And why does the model
behave if it perfectly

271
00:14:20,350 --> 00:14:23,350
understands the dynamics?

272
00:14:23,350 --> 00:14:28,430
Let's say the model learned
the monkey eating banana.

273
00:14:28,430 --> 00:14:34,190
If model is truly understanding
this eating mechanism,

274
00:14:34,190 --> 00:14:39,720
the model might be able to infer
what is happening to banana

275
00:14:39,720 --> 00:14:44,120
when we cover the
invisible cloth on banana.

276
00:14:44,120 --> 00:14:46,600
So even though you're
not seeing the banana,

277
00:14:46,600 --> 00:14:50,200
if you see a monkey moving
their mouth constantly,

278
00:14:50,200 --> 00:14:53,280
you can imagine, oh, a
banana might get shorter.

279
00:14:53,280 --> 00:14:59,640
And vice versa, if you make
the monkey invisible and banana

280
00:14:59,640 --> 00:15:04,040
is disappearing, you
can infer that a monkey

281
00:15:04,040 --> 00:15:05,640
is eating something.

282
00:15:05,640 --> 00:15:08,760
This is very core
motivation and very core

283
00:15:08,760 --> 00:15:10,540
explanation of Causal-JEPA.

284
00:15:10,540 --> 00:15:15,200
This is really relevant of
what I'm doing right now.

285
00:15:15,200 --> 00:15:18,120
And if you think of
the previous example,

286
00:15:18,120 --> 00:15:20,000
it doesn't have to be--
the predictor doesn't

287
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:23,160
have to be Causal Transformer.

288
00:15:23,160 --> 00:15:25,660
As far as we are seeing
the only history,

289
00:15:25,660 --> 00:15:28,640
we're OK to use multiple
time-step history.

290
00:15:28,640 --> 00:15:31,420
So we're using a bidirectional
transformer here.

291
00:15:31,420 --> 00:15:34,560
I would like to mention that.

292
00:15:34,560 --> 00:15:40,050
And let's see how
the model actually

293
00:15:40,050 --> 00:15:42,730
implements this, the monkey
and banana mechanism,

294
00:15:42,730 --> 00:15:45,010
in the transformer.

295
00:15:45,010 --> 00:15:48,210
Let's say we have
a nicely aligned

296
00:15:48,210 --> 00:15:52,010
representation for each object.

297
00:15:52,010 --> 00:15:55,590
And we include these object
states to the representation.

298
00:15:55,590 --> 00:15:59,670
For example, let's say our
lookback history is four frames.

299
00:15:59,670 --> 00:16:00,950
So you see four frames.

300
00:16:00,950 --> 00:16:05,810
And next frame
prediction will happen.

301
00:16:05,810 --> 00:16:09,630
But because this is not a causal
autoregressive transformer,

302
00:16:09,630 --> 00:16:13,090
this is a bidirectional
ViT-style transformer,

303
00:16:13,090 --> 00:16:16,150
we need a placeholder for
this future tokens as well.

304
00:16:16,150 --> 00:16:20,010
So we use this as
a masked token.

305
00:16:20,010 --> 00:16:30,020
And now so our goal is predict
this masked token correctly.

306
00:16:30,020 --> 00:16:36,180
And for example, each row means
the evolution of each object.

307
00:16:36,180 --> 00:16:38,860
Now, as I told you before,
I want to mask something.

308
00:16:38,860 --> 00:16:41,700
Here, blue dots are
observable slots,

309
00:16:41,700 --> 00:16:45,020
and the yellow one
is masked slot.

310
00:16:45,020 --> 00:16:50,420
And let's imagine that we
have this masked slot here.

311
00:16:50,420 --> 00:16:51,360
What should model do?

312
00:16:51,360 --> 00:16:54,380
What is the easiest
way for model

313
00:16:54,380 --> 00:16:59,220
to have a reasonably low
loss and just predict

314
00:16:59,220 --> 00:17:00,740
the masked token?

315
00:17:00,740 --> 00:17:03,560
Maybe just average the
previous and the next token,

316
00:17:03,560 --> 00:17:05,859
just like doing interpolation.

317
00:17:05,859 --> 00:17:07,319
But that is what we want.

318
00:17:07,319 --> 00:17:11,500
What we want is like a learn
the object interaction.

319
00:17:11,500 --> 00:17:16,000
So like the previous example of
monkey, I just mask everything.

320
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,700
This is a bit aggressive
way of masking.

321
00:17:18,700 --> 00:17:21,280
But now the model
doesn't have shortcut.

322
00:17:21,280 --> 00:17:25,180
It needs to infer the other
slots to correctly infer

323
00:17:25,180 --> 00:17:27,760
the current state
or the masked state.

324
00:17:31,990 --> 00:17:37,470
But what if we want to mask
two objects at the same time?

325
00:17:37,470 --> 00:17:40,670
This can happen, because
in slot attention,

326
00:17:40,670 --> 00:17:43,370
you have to fix the
maximum number of slots.

327
00:17:43,370 --> 00:17:46,190
The number of slots doesn't
vary during training,

328
00:17:46,190 --> 00:17:52,450
so you give plenty amount of
slot to give a model freedom OK,

329
00:17:52,450 --> 00:17:55,670
what information should
define as a slot?

330
00:17:55,670 --> 00:17:57,430
And sometimes in the scene--

331
00:17:57,430 --> 00:17:59,090
for example, we
define eight slots,

332
00:17:59,090 --> 00:18:02,070
but we only have three
objects in the scene.

333
00:18:02,070 --> 00:18:04,930
Then just masking
only one object,

334
00:18:04,930 --> 00:18:08,550
only one slot might
not be enough.

335
00:18:08,550 --> 00:18:11,350
And then now we want to
put this into transformer.

336
00:18:11,350 --> 00:18:14,990
And we have deflection, so we
have to do positional encoding

337
00:18:14,990 --> 00:18:17,470
before then.

338
00:18:17,470 --> 00:18:22,030
OK, there's no problem by
temporal positional encoding.

339
00:18:22,030 --> 00:18:25,470
But when we are trying to do
positional encoding in the slot

340
00:18:25,470 --> 00:18:29,310
axis, there's a problem, because
what object-centric models are

341
00:18:29,310 --> 00:18:33,970
doing is they do not define
the order of the objects,

342
00:18:33,970 --> 00:18:41,630
but rather the object-centric
models are permutationally

343
00:18:41,630 --> 00:18:47,570
equivalent depends with
respect to the object orders.

344
00:18:50,110 --> 00:18:55,190
So this is basically not
list but set of objects.

345
00:18:55,190 --> 00:18:57,750
And then if you are
masking multiple objects,

346
00:18:57,750 --> 00:19:00,270
the model might not
know what to predict,

347
00:19:00,270 --> 00:19:05,630
because these slots are
not having object identity.

348
00:19:05,630 --> 00:19:10,950
So the video slot attentions can
keep the temporal consistency

349
00:19:10,950 --> 00:19:12,530
inside the video itself.

350
00:19:12,530 --> 00:19:15,110
For example, Video A
have the consistency

351
00:19:15,110 --> 00:19:17,430
with the object order.

352
00:19:17,430 --> 00:19:20,790
But we cannot guarantee that
Video A and Video B are having

353
00:19:20,790 --> 00:19:24,030
the same order of objects.

354
00:19:24,030 --> 00:19:30,360
So now what we do is we do not
mask the very first time step.

355
00:19:30,360 --> 00:19:32,840
In this case, time
step t minus 3.

356
00:19:32,840 --> 00:19:37,920
And we use this information
as a slot identity.

357
00:19:37,920 --> 00:19:39,920
And what we are
doing is we define

358
00:19:39,920 --> 00:19:45,600
each masked token with the
identity token plus learnable

359
00:19:45,600 --> 00:19:50,520
masked token with the
positional encoding.

360
00:19:50,520 --> 00:19:52,400
And this is less
aggressive because you

361
00:19:52,400 --> 00:19:54,800
have an initial
condition of each object.

362
00:19:54,800 --> 00:19:59,060
So it makes more sense when
you predict the masked tokens.

363
00:20:02,360 --> 00:20:04,080
And the other part
I want to talk about

364
00:20:04,080 --> 00:20:05,260
is action conditioning.

365
00:20:05,260 --> 00:20:09,160
The world model should condition
on the action properly.

366
00:20:09,160 --> 00:20:11,880
And what the DINO
World Model is doing

367
00:20:11,880 --> 00:20:17,600
is they concatenate the action
embedding behind the patch

368
00:20:17,600 --> 00:20:18,100
embedding.

369
00:20:18,100 --> 00:20:24,280
For example, DINOv2-Small has
384 dimension of the features.

370
00:20:24,280 --> 00:20:29,410
And let's say we have 10
dimensional action embedding.

371
00:20:29,410 --> 00:20:31,930
And what DINO World Model
do is they duplicate

372
00:20:31,930 --> 00:20:36,330
it to make the number
of the patches,

373
00:20:36,330 --> 00:20:38,970
and they just put
the action embedding

374
00:20:38,970 --> 00:20:41,870
after the patch representation.

375
00:20:41,870 --> 00:20:46,330
So now it's like 894
per patch because we

376
00:20:46,330 --> 00:20:50,690
added this action embedding
after the patch representation.

377
00:20:50,690 --> 00:20:54,810
But this is not really
optimal way, I think,

378
00:20:54,810 --> 00:20:58,850
because what we want to
learn is something like this.

379
00:20:58,850 --> 00:21:04,770
Why don't we consider action
as another node of the graph?

380
00:21:04,770 --> 00:21:08,970
The Causal-JEPA does not
recover any true causal graph,

381
00:21:08,970 --> 00:21:12,530
but its motivation is
grounded in the causal graph.

382
00:21:12,530 --> 00:21:16,090
So for example, because
we define each object

383
00:21:16,090 --> 00:21:20,570
representation, those are
playing a role of the nodes.

384
00:21:20,570 --> 00:21:26,620
And we also try to use this
action as one of the node.

385
00:21:26,620 --> 00:21:30,260
So in this current
situation, the action

386
00:21:30,260 --> 00:21:33,180
is added like
something like this,

387
00:21:33,180 --> 00:21:39,300
not as a part of the
feature representation.

388
00:21:39,300 --> 00:21:44,420
So this is the architecture
of the Causal-JEPA.

389
00:21:44,420 --> 00:21:46,960
So to sum up, you have
the history frames.

390
00:21:46,960 --> 00:21:49,460
And you put this into the
object-centric encoder

391
00:21:49,460 --> 00:21:52,300
to get the object
representation.

392
00:21:52,300 --> 00:21:55,780
And then you select some
amount of objects to mask,

393
00:21:55,780 --> 00:21:56,980
and you mask it.

394
00:21:56,980 --> 00:22:01,380
And you put this
into predictor, which

395
00:22:01,380 --> 00:22:03,900
is bidirectional
transformer with the action.

396
00:22:03,900 --> 00:22:07,800
And then you're predicting
every masked tokens.

397
00:22:10,940 --> 00:22:16,180
And now we will
see some results.

398
00:22:16,180 --> 00:22:19,300
I did three experiments
based on our goal.

399
00:22:19,300 --> 00:22:22,120
Our goal is understanding
the object dynamics.

400
00:22:22,120 --> 00:22:26,350
And the first thing is reasoning
on the counterfactual questions.

401
00:22:26,350 --> 00:22:28,370
And the second thing is
planning and control.

402
00:22:28,370 --> 00:22:32,310
And third is physical
implausibility.

403
00:22:32,310 --> 00:22:35,270
So in the CLEVRER,
we did an experiment

404
00:22:35,270 --> 00:22:38,050
with the other existing
models as well.

405
00:22:38,050 --> 00:22:42,350
But what I want to highlight
is the model without masking.

406
00:22:42,350 --> 00:22:45,150
What I want to say
here is the performance

407
00:22:45,150 --> 00:22:48,730
is not because we use the
object-centric representation,

408
00:22:48,730 --> 00:22:54,670
but the core is masking,
like in the banana example.

409
00:22:54,670 --> 00:22:59,510
So if you see the C-JEPA result,
not only average accuracy

410
00:22:59,510 --> 00:23:00,030
is better.

411
00:23:00,030 --> 00:23:03,470
You can see there's
a clearly clear gain

412
00:23:03,470 --> 00:23:05,230
in the counterfactual question.

413
00:23:05,230 --> 00:23:07,270
This counterfactual
question is very well

414
00:23:07,270 --> 00:23:10,310
resonated with our
original motivation

415
00:23:10,310 --> 00:23:12,570
because the counterfactual
is asking something like,

416
00:23:12,570 --> 00:23:17,410
oh, what if this
object doesn't exist?

417
00:23:17,410 --> 00:23:19,670
What if this object exists?

418
00:23:19,670 --> 00:23:20,810
Something like this.

419
00:23:20,810 --> 00:23:24,410
So you have to understand how
the object interact each other.

420
00:23:28,350 --> 00:23:32,110
And the next example
is Push-T planning.

421
00:23:32,110 --> 00:23:36,430
The agent tried to control the
object to reach the goal state.

422
00:23:36,430 --> 00:23:41,350
And here I want to
emphasize the efficiency.

423
00:23:41,350 --> 00:23:43,990
If you can see the DINO
World Model baseline,

424
00:23:43,990 --> 00:23:49,550
you have 196 patches, if you
imagine you are having 2 to 4

425
00:23:49,550 --> 00:23:51,190
by 2 to 4 images.

426
00:23:51,190 --> 00:23:55,950
And each patch should have a
384 dimension of the features.

427
00:23:55,950 --> 00:23:59,190
And if you're using
object-centric representation,

428
00:23:59,190 --> 00:24:04,590
the number of tokens are
significantly less than that.

429
00:24:04,590 --> 00:24:09,990
And because now we have the
clear semantic from each token,

430
00:24:09,990 --> 00:24:13,670
the feature doesn't
have to be super large.

431
00:24:13,670 --> 00:24:15,310
If you think of--
there's nothing much

432
00:24:15,310 --> 00:24:17,750
to include in the object.

433
00:24:17,750 --> 00:24:23,540
For example, texture. color,
shape, rotational state,

434
00:24:23,540 --> 00:24:24,765
and location.

435
00:24:24,765 --> 00:24:25,640
That's not that much.

436
00:24:25,640 --> 00:24:31,240
We don't need like a really
huge representation space.

437
00:24:31,240 --> 00:24:36,960
So after we only put in
object-centric representation

438
00:24:36,960 --> 00:24:39,560
instead of the
patch representation

439
00:24:39,560 --> 00:24:42,640
in the DINO World
Model, its performance

440
00:24:42,640 --> 00:24:45,560
actually drops a lot.

441
00:24:45,560 --> 00:24:48,720
And this can be true
because DINO World Model

442
00:24:48,720 --> 00:24:50,440
used the causal transformer.

443
00:24:50,440 --> 00:24:52,660
And we use bidirectional
transformer, right?

444
00:24:52,660 --> 00:24:54,960
And the object-centered
representation if you think

445
00:24:54,960 --> 00:24:58,880
of doesn't necessarily have
to include the velocity

446
00:24:58,880 --> 00:25:02,480
or acceleration stuff, because
you cannot define those

447
00:25:02,480 --> 00:25:06,680
properties by only looking
at the single static image.

448
00:25:06,680 --> 00:25:12,120
So this drops the
performance a lot.

449
00:25:12,120 --> 00:25:16,800
And after we changed the
action-conditioning method,

450
00:25:16,800 --> 00:25:18,770
treating them as
a separated node,

451
00:25:18,770 --> 00:25:23,170
and we change the transformer to
the bidirectional transformer,

452
00:25:23,170 --> 00:25:25,770
the performance
gain is significant.

453
00:25:25,770 --> 00:25:31,450
It gains this 15% of
absolute percentages.

454
00:25:31,450 --> 00:25:37,250
And after masking, compared
to the OC, Object-Centric DINO

455
00:25:37,250 --> 00:25:41,010
World Model, you gain 28%,
which is pretty large.

456
00:25:41,010 --> 00:25:43,770
And compared to the
OC-JEPA, the only difference

457
00:25:43,770 --> 00:25:45,330
is object masking.

458
00:25:45,330 --> 00:25:48,690
And this object masking
actually helps the model

459
00:25:48,690 --> 00:25:50,350
to understand these dynamics.

460
00:25:53,730 --> 00:25:57,850
And this is the effect of
the action conditioning.

461
00:25:57,850 --> 00:26:02,050
As I told you before, the latent
concatenation in the red line

462
00:26:02,050 --> 00:26:04,250
denotes the
action-conditioning method

463
00:26:04,250 --> 00:26:06,250
based on the DINO World Model.

464
00:26:06,250 --> 00:26:10,490
But after we treating this
action as a separated node,

465
00:26:10,490 --> 00:26:13,430
it clearly shows the margin.

466
00:26:17,500 --> 00:26:20,120
And there's an additional
experiment on PHYRE.

467
00:26:20,120 --> 00:26:23,640
PHYRE is-- among three
data sets we have,

468
00:26:23,640 --> 00:26:25,920
it's like most
complicated dynamics.

469
00:26:25,920 --> 00:26:30,380
And there's a lot of
formulation configuration.

470
00:26:30,380 --> 00:26:32,900
And you need to
learn precisely what

471
00:26:32,900 --> 00:26:35,260
is happening in the scenario.

472
00:26:35,260 --> 00:26:39,460
And when I compare the
OC-JEPA and C-JEPA,

473
00:26:39,460 --> 00:26:43,540
OC-JEPA often generates some
physically implausible scene.

474
00:26:43,540 --> 00:26:48,340
You see like the bar is
floating below the fixed

475
00:26:48,340 --> 00:26:50,540
bar, which doesn't make sense.

476
00:26:50,540 --> 00:26:54,280
And this can be done by
just learning correlation.

477
00:26:54,280 --> 00:26:58,127
When two bars are closed,
they just stay there.

478
00:26:58,127 --> 00:26:58,960
But that's not true.

479
00:26:58,960 --> 00:27:01,660
That's not how physics works.

480
00:27:01,660 --> 00:27:05,040
So by the training
method of object masking,

481
00:27:05,040 --> 00:27:08,900
you keep asking the question
to the model, what would

482
00:27:08,900 --> 00:27:12,220
happen if this doesn't exist?

483
00:27:12,220 --> 00:27:16,110
What should you consider to
predict the masked token?

484
00:27:16,110 --> 00:27:20,350
It can learn the true dynamics.

485
00:27:20,350 --> 00:27:25,150
And this is the
attention probing

486
00:27:25,150 --> 00:27:29,070
for this previous example.

487
00:27:29,070 --> 00:27:30,950
You can see the
failure is actually

488
00:27:30,950 --> 00:27:34,910
coming from attending to
the wrong irrelevant object.

489
00:27:34,910 --> 00:27:39,430
So for example, OC-JEPA
relies on the cup, which

490
00:27:39,430 --> 00:27:47,190
contains the blue ball,
and the C-JEPA conditioned

491
00:27:47,190 --> 00:27:52,550
on the right bar to
predict its future state.

492
00:27:52,550 --> 00:27:57,010
And here, the Causal-JEPA,
the terminology causal can--

493
00:27:59,650 --> 00:28:01,310
it can sense for many things.

494
00:28:01,310 --> 00:28:04,350
But here, we use the causal
as a temporally directed

495
00:28:04,350 --> 00:28:06,150
predictive dependencies,
which means

496
00:28:06,150 --> 00:28:09,830
that because we're predicting
the future from the history,

497
00:28:09,830 --> 00:28:14,590
the edge is directed.

498
00:28:14,590 --> 00:28:17,190
And to predict the
masked token, you

499
00:28:17,190 --> 00:28:19,250
need to attend to
the relevant object.

500
00:28:19,250 --> 00:28:20,830
So we call it
temporally directed

501
00:28:20,830 --> 00:28:22,350
predictive dependencies.

502
00:28:22,350 --> 00:28:24,830
This is not the very
conventional and traditional way

503
00:28:24,830 --> 00:28:30,910
of defining causal, but recent
modern causal machine learning

504
00:28:30,910 --> 00:28:36,470
is this kind of
definition as well.

505
00:28:36,470 --> 00:28:42,430
And here, to go back to the
role of the object masking,

506
00:28:42,430 --> 00:28:45,430
we say this predictor finds
influence neighborhood.

507
00:28:45,430 --> 00:28:48,150
Influence neighborhood--
we just define its name.

508
00:28:48,150 --> 00:28:51,410
But it's just a
predictably sufficient set.

509
00:28:51,410 --> 00:28:53,310
So it's like a
minimal set that it

510
00:28:53,310 --> 00:28:58,590
needs to predict the
masked token correctly.

511
00:28:58,590 --> 00:29:00,670
And this can be true.

512
00:29:00,670 --> 00:29:03,550
More formally, this can
be true for assumptions.

513
00:29:03,550 --> 00:29:05,630
The first thing is we
do not assume there's

514
00:29:05,630 --> 00:29:07,950
an instantaneous relationship.

515
00:29:07,950 --> 00:29:12,720
And the second assumption is
that every training instances

516
00:29:12,720 --> 00:29:15,080
should share the same mechanism.

517
00:29:15,080 --> 00:29:21,680
For example, the gravity
should not change along videos.

518
00:29:21,680 --> 00:29:27,680
There's a governing rule
applied in the whole data set.

519
00:29:27,680 --> 00:29:30,780
And the third assumption is
object-aligned representation.

520
00:29:30,780 --> 00:29:34,900
This is the most tricky
in the practical sense,

521
00:29:34,900 --> 00:29:38,480
because this assumes that our
object-centric representation is

522
00:29:38,480 --> 00:29:41,360
constant throughout the video.

523
00:29:41,360 --> 00:29:44,300
It should not getting swapped.

524
00:29:44,300 --> 00:29:47,240
The object should not be split
into the different slots.

525
00:29:47,240 --> 00:29:51,960
And our representation should
reflect the scenario faithfully.

526
00:29:51,960 --> 00:29:54,640
And the fourth is
history sufficiency.

527
00:29:54,640 --> 00:29:57,300
This is because we
use finite history.

528
00:29:57,300 --> 00:29:59,480
For example, we see
four previous frames

529
00:29:59,480 --> 00:30:01,520
and predict the next frame.

530
00:30:01,520 --> 00:30:03,480
And this four history
frame should be

531
00:30:03,480 --> 00:30:08,200
enough to predict the future.

532
00:30:08,200 --> 00:30:13,650
And to make this causal
machine learning to practical,

533
00:30:13,650 --> 00:30:16,205
we do not assume
first-order Markov process.

534
00:30:16,205 --> 00:30:18,330
As I told before, the
object-centric representation

535
00:30:18,330 --> 00:30:22,530
usually does not follow the
first-order Markov process.

536
00:30:22,530 --> 00:30:26,090
And we allow confounder.

537
00:30:26,090 --> 00:30:29,290
The confounder makes things
really tricky because we cannot

538
00:30:29,290 --> 00:30:32,330
recover the true
causal graph usually

539
00:30:32,330 --> 00:30:33,770
because of the confounder.

540
00:30:33,770 --> 00:30:36,610
But in the object-centric
representation

541
00:30:36,610 --> 00:30:40,590
in the real world,
this is inevitable.

542
00:30:43,650 --> 00:30:48,870
So I would like to answer
this questions really quickly.

543
00:30:48,870 --> 00:30:52,930
What happens if object-centric
representation is not faithful?

544
00:30:52,930 --> 00:30:57,810
A little bit is fine because
still, masking object slots

545
00:30:57,810 --> 00:30:59,970
is still some kind
of inductive bias,

546
00:30:59,970 --> 00:31:03,170
even though our
masking is not perfect.

547
00:31:03,170 --> 00:31:13,060
So a little minor--

548
00:31:13,060 --> 00:31:16,640
like the wrong model can be
OK, but if it's really bad,

549
00:31:16,640 --> 00:31:17,480
it doesn't work.

550
00:31:20,260 --> 00:31:22,560
Is there any way to recover
the true causal graph?

551
00:31:22,560 --> 00:31:25,220
No in our method.

552
00:31:25,220 --> 00:31:28,060
There's confounders, so we
cannot recover the true causal

553
00:31:28,060 --> 00:31:28,560
graph.

554
00:31:28,560 --> 00:31:30,060
And also, sometimes
it's really hard

555
00:31:30,060 --> 00:31:34,220
to define what is the true
causal graph in many scenarios.

556
00:31:34,220 --> 00:31:36,900
How to select the number
of objects to mask?

557
00:31:36,900 --> 00:31:40,720
This is a good question, because
we have a fixed number of slots,

558
00:31:40,720 --> 00:31:44,460
and the number of actual objects
are varying depend on which

559
00:31:44,460 --> 00:31:46,780
frame are we looking at.

560
00:31:46,780 --> 00:31:53,020
The ideal amount is just
only one foreground object

561
00:31:53,020 --> 00:31:55,100
without the background slots.

562
00:31:55,100 --> 00:31:59,080
But we sometimes cannot
control this really well,

563
00:31:59,080 --> 00:32:06,260
so I would say decide the number
of masks based on the data

564
00:32:06,260 --> 00:32:07,160
statistics.

565
00:32:07,160 --> 00:32:13,410
Just guess your perfect
number of the mask.

566
00:32:13,410 --> 00:32:18,190
And you should sweep a bit
to find the perfect number.

567
00:32:18,190 --> 00:32:20,270
And the limitation--
the largest limitation

568
00:32:20,270 --> 00:32:22,450
is coming from the
object-centric encoder.

569
00:32:22,450 --> 00:32:24,190
The object-centric
representation

570
00:32:24,190 --> 00:32:28,550
does not work really well
on the occlusion situation.

571
00:32:28,550 --> 00:32:32,190
And, in the middle of
the video, some objects

572
00:32:32,190 --> 00:32:33,530
can appear and disappear.

573
00:32:33,530 --> 00:32:38,190
But this slot attention cannot
handle this scenario really

574
00:32:38,190 --> 00:32:38,830
well.

575
00:32:38,830 --> 00:32:42,830
So this is some pain
point of this model.

576
00:32:42,830 --> 00:32:47,330
And finally, we got back to this
three components of world model

577
00:32:47,330 --> 00:32:48,190
again.

578
00:32:48,190 --> 00:32:50,670
In the beginning
of the talk, I said

579
00:32:50,670 --> 00:32:53,310
there's a three component
that we should consider

580
00:32:53,310 --> 00:32:55,350
to make a good world model.

581
00:32:55,350 --> 00:32:57,910
And for the good
state representation,

582
00:32:57,910 --> 00:33:00,910
we use this object
representation.

583
00:33:00,910 --> 00:33:03,470
And for a good
transition model, we

584
00:33:03,470 --> 00:33:06,990
did object masking to
let the model learn

585
00:33:06,990 --> 00:33:10,590
the predictive sufficiency.

586
00:33:10,590 --> 00:33:16,390
And about the dynamics
model, we tweak the method

587
00:33:16,390 --> 00:33:17,910
to condition the action.

588
00:33:17,910 --> 00:33:24,470
So we treat this action
variables as a separated node.

589
00:33:24,470 --> 00:33:27,730
I think this is all of
the Causal-JEPA part.

590
00:33:27,730 --> 00:33:31,850
And let me quickly put to Lucas.

591
00:33:36,110 --> 00:33:37,590
So hi, everyone.

592
00:33:37,590 --> 00:33:39,030
My name is Lucas.

593
00:33:39,030 --> 00:33:42,950
I'm a third year
PhD student at Mila,

594
00:33:42,950 --> 00:33:47,030
advised by Damien Scieur,
who is researcher in Samsung.

595
00:33:47,030 --> 00:33:50,110
But I work closely with
Randall Balestriero,

596
00:33:50,110 --> 00:33:52,430
who is prof at Brown.

597
00:33:52,430 --> 00:33:55,150
And the work I'm going
to talk about today

598
00:33:55,150 --> 00:33:59,555
is the work I done in
collaboration with Quentin Le

599
00:33:59,555 --> 00:34:04,580
Lidec, who is postdoc at NYU;
with Yann LeCun, Damien; Yann

600
00:34:04,580 --> 00:34:07,800
LeCun and Randall.

601
00:34:07,800 --> 00:34:10,520
So today, what I'm going
to talk about briefly

602
00:34:10,520 --> 00:34:14,960
is how to make this whole
world model stuff and JEPA

603
00:34:14,960 --> 00:34:17,880
stuff pretty simple to train.

604
00:34:17,880 --> 00:34:23,580
And I'm not going to tell you
again what is the world model.

605
00:34:23,580 --> 00:34:25,880
I think Hazel did
it pretty well.

606
00:34:25,880 --> 00:34:29,639
So I'm going to go
directly to what we did.

607
00:34:29,639 --> 00:34:31,639
So just before
that, I would like

608
00:34:31,639 --> 00:34:33,219
to talk about a big problem.

609
00:34:33,219 --> 00:34:38,760
So as Hazel say, JEPA aim
to learn representation

610
00:34:38,760 --> 00:34:40,139
in abstract space.

611
00:34:40,139 --> 00:34:42,840
And so it's directly
in opposition

612
00:34:42,840 --> 00:34:45,699
to generative modeling,
wherein generative modeling,

613
00:34:45,699 --> 00:34:49,060
what we try to do is you try
to model your input space.

614
00:34:49,060 --> 00:34:51,679
Basically, you try to learn a
representation of your input

615
00:34:51,679 --> 00:34:55,020
space and try to do stuff in
your input pace-- input space,

616
00:34:55,020 --> 00:34:55,719
sorry.

617
00:34:55,719 --> 00:34:59,940
And so JEPA say that this
is for most of your tasks,

618
00:34:59,940 --> 00:35:01,720
not desirable.

619
00:35:01,720 --> 00:35:05,130
For instance, if you do
self-driving car application,

620
00:35:05,130 --> 00:35:08,290
you most likely
don't care to model

621
00:35:08,290 --> 00:35:12,150
the movement of the leaf
of the tree in the road.

622
00:35:12,150 --> 00:35:13,630
You don't care to model that.

623
00:35:13,630 --> 00:35:16,682
If you do generative
modeling, by definition,

624
00:35:16,682 --> 00:35:18,890
you will have to model that
because you want to model

625
00:35:18,890 --> 00:35:20,430
all the detail of your input.

626
00:35:20,430 --> 00:35:23,530
So your loss is going
to give signal for that.

627
00:35:23,530 --> 00:35:27,730
So what JEPA propose is to
first encode all your input

628
00:35:27,730 --> 00:35:29,570
into an abstract
space, typically

629
00:35:29,570 --> 00:35:32,010
with an encoder or
neural net, and then

630
00:35:32,010 --> 00:35:34,050
try to model the dynamic--

631
00:35:34,050 --> 00:35:39,550
the dynamics-- sorry-- of your
space in that latent space.

632
00:35:39,550 --> 00:35:42,830
So it's pretty nice
when you say like that.

633
00:35:42,830 --> 00:35:46,130
But if you look on the
right the image I put,

634
00:35:46,130 --> 00:35:49,910
if you just do that, you suffer
from what people call collapse.

635
00:35:49,910 --> 00:35:52,190
And so what is collapse?

636
00:35:52,190 --> 00:35:57,310
It's a failure mode where you
can see that if I do nothing,

637
00:35:57,310 --> 00:36:01,340
I don't put constraint on the
distribution of my embeddings,

638
00:36:01,340 --> 00:36:03,540
your model on the
right can simply

639
00:36:03,540 --> 00:36:08,620
learn to disregard the input and
just produce a constant vector.

640
00:36:08,620 --> 00:36:13,260
And so you can minimize the
prediction loss in the latent

641
00:36:13,260 --> 00:36:16,620
space just by saying, OK, I
am going to encode everything

642
00:36:16,620 --> 00:36:18,900
as a constant vector like 0.

643
00:36:18,900 --> 00:36:20,940
And then it's trivially
easy to predict

644
00:36:20,940 --> 00:36:22,980
what is going to be the next--

645
00:36:22,980 --> 00:36:25,420
state is going to
just be 0 again.

646
00:36:25,420 --> 00:36:28,380
And so the whole
research on JEPA--

647
00:36:28,380 --> 00:36:30,180
a big part of the
research on JEPA

648
00:36:30,180 --> 00:36:33,660
is how do you avoid
that collapse.

649
00:36:33,660 --> 00:36:36,540
So as Hazel said
before, I should

650
00:36:36,540 --> 00:36:40,740
add a Causal-JEPA in that
previous JEPA recipe as well.

651
00:36:40,740 --> 00:36:43,500
You have V-JEPA that try to do--

652
00:36:43,500 --> 00:36:47,780
that try to avoid that collapse
with exponential moving average.

653
00:36:47,780 --> 00:36:50,980
You have DINO World Model
that uses a pretrained encoder

654
00:36:50,980 --> 00:36:53,780
basically to avoid the
collapse, because if you

655
00:36:53,780 --> 00:36:56,340
use a pretrained encoder
and you froze it,

656
00:36:56,340 --> 00:36:59,755
you can produce
nontrivial embedding,

657
00:36:59,755 --> 00:37:02,130
and you can learn the dynamics
directly in the embedding.

658
00:37:02,130 --> 00:37:05,790
So it's essentially supervised
learning in our model.

659
00:37:05,790 --> 00:37:10,150
And then you have PLDM that
try everything end to end.

660
00:37:10,150 --> 00:37:13,310
So they train the encoder or
the predictor and everything end

661
00:37:13,310 --> 00:37:15,950
to end and their
action-conditioned model

662
00:37:15,950 --> 00:37:16,710
as well.

663
00:37:16,710 --> 00:37:19,790
But they use something
called VICReg.

664
00:37:19,790 --> 00:37:21,330
And what is VICReg?

665
00:37:21,330 --> 00:37:25,270
It's just an anti-collapse
regularization term

666
00:37:25,270 --> 00:37:30,790
that try to make the covariance
matrix of your features,

667
00:37:30,790 --> 00:37:34,830
of your latent
feature's identity.

668
00:37:34,830 --> 00:37:38,230
So basically, you
want each dimension

669
00:37:38,230 --> 00:37:43,390
to be decorrelated from the
other of your feature space,

670
00:37:43,390 --> 00:37:45,370
except for the
covariance matrix.

671
00:37:45,370 --> 00:37:48,490
The diagonal line
is one typically.

672
00:37:48,490 --> 00:37:51,870
So you have a
positive correlation.

673
00:37:51,870 --> 00:37:55,210
The problem with
PLDM is as you--

674
00:37:55,210 --> 00:37:57,700
as you can see on the
slide, you need to apply.

675
00:37:57,700 --> 00:37:59,420
So one term for the variance.

676
00:37:59,420 --> 00:38:02,960
One term for the
covariance minimization.

677
00:38:02,960 --> 00:38:05,020
You need to do that
spatially and temporally.

678
00:38:05,020 --> 00:38:06,360
So you have four terms.

679
00:38:06,360 --> 00:38:08,760
They add an additional
inverse dynamics

680
00:38:08,760 --> 00:38:12,300
loss to try to gain more
information about the action,

681
00:38:12,300 --> 00:38:12,920
whatever.

682
00:38:12,920 --> 00:38:14,080
It's 5.

683
00:38:14,080 --> 00:38:18,040
Plus, you have the
prediction in the future.

684
00:38:18,040 --> 00:38:20,100
The L2 minimization
you see on the right.

685
00:38:20,100 --> 00:38:24,120
So your predictor try to predict
the future in the latent space.

686
00:38:24,120 --> 00:38:26,400
And you compare that with
what your encoder say

687
00:38:26,400 --> 00:38:28,420
about the future should be.

688
00:38:28,420 --> 00:38:29,990
And so it gives you
a six-term loss,

689
00:38:29,990 --> 00:38:32,240
so it's pretty difficult to
tune that because you have

690
00:38:32,240 --> 00:38:34,600
six hyperparameters to tune.

691
00:38:34,600 --> 00:38:38,380
And that's why we
propose LeWorldModel.

692
00:38:38,380 --> 00:38:39,420
So what is LeWorldModel?

693
00:38:39,420 --> 00:38:43,780
It's just a simple JEPA
that doesn't use any tricks.

694
00:38:43,780 --> 00:38:46,380
So there is no Exponential
Moving Average, no masking,

695
00:38:46,380 --> 00:38:49,200
no stop gradient, no
pretrained encoder,

696
00:38:49,200 --> 00:38:50,860
and also no unstable loss.

697
00:38:50,860 --> 00:38:51,360
Why?

698
00:38:51,360 --> 00:38:54,720
Because we have a
single hyperparameter.

699
00:38:54,720 --> 00:38:58,380
And we make the model such that
it's only 16 million parameter.

700
00:38:58,380 --> 00:39:01,360
So it means you can train
that on a single GPU.

701
00:39:01,360 --> 00:39:03,380
It's fully end to
end from raw pixels,

702
00:39:03,380 --> 00:39:06,360
so there is no
auxiliary information

703
00:39:06,360 --> 00:39:09,640
such as proprioception
information or whatever.

704
00:39:09,640 --> 00:39:12,240
And we observe that
it's 50 times faster

705
00:39:12,240 --> 00:39:16,960
than the LeWorldModel
for doing planning.

706
00:39:16,960 --> 00:39:19,060
So basically, what
is LeWorldModel?

707
00:39:19,060 --> 00:39:22,440
It's basically JEPA
in its pure essence.

708
00:39:22,440 --> 00:39:25,440
So what you do is you
take an observation ot,

709
00:39:25,440 --> 00:39:28,960
an observation ot plus 1,
so the next observation.

710
00:39:28,960 --> 00:39:30,360
You process-- [COUGHS]

711
00:39:30,360 --> 00:39:30,860
sorry.

712
00:39:30,860 --> 00:39:34,120
You process both of them
through a shared encoder.

713
00:39:34,120 --> 00:39:38,840
And you get a representation
zt and zt plus 1.

714
00:39:38,840 --> 00:39:40,360
What you're going
to do in the task

715
00:39:40,360 --> 00:39:44,840
is you will use zt and
the action at step t

716
00:39:44,840 --> 00:39:47,780
and try to learn a predictor
to model the dynamics.

717
00:39:47,780 --> 00:39:49,680
So you're going to try
to predict the future.

718
00:39:49,680 --> 00:39:52,960
So you have an estimation of
the future called zt plus 1.

719
00:39:52,960 --> 00:39:55,210
And you compare that
to what your encoder

720
00:39:55,210 --> 00:39:57,130
predict for the next state.

721
00:39:57,130 --> 00:39:59,690
You use Mean Squared
Error, and you

722
00:39:59,690 --> 00:40:03,330
use what we call SIGReg
to avoid collapse.

723
00:40:03,330 --> 00:40:05,370
What is nice is
that basically what

724
00:40:05,370 --> 00:40:08,910
I told you about before with
JEPA is literally what you see.

725
00:40:08,910 --> 00:40:11,150
And the code is exactly
what I just described.

726
00:40:11,150 --> 00:40:13,190
So if you look at the
pseudocode on the right,

727
00:40:13,190 --> 00:40:15,150
it's actually not
that much pseudocode.

728
00:40:15,150 --> 00:40:17,010
It's literally the true code.

729
00:40:17,010 --> 00:40:17,790
You encode.

730
00:40:17,790 --> 00:40:18,490
You predict.

731
00:40:18,490 --> 00:40:21,850
You compute your prediction,
your future prediction error.

732
00:40:21,850 --> 00:40:24,270
And you use SIGReg
to avoid collapse.

733
00:40:27,240 --> 00:40:30,810
So you can see that at the
bottom line at the return,

734
00:40:30,810 --> 00:40:32,870
I have a single
hyperparameter lambda.

735
00:40:32,870 --> 00:40:34,975
So this is the only
stuff you need to tune.

736
00:40:34,975 --> 00:40:36,850
And because you have a
single hyperparameter,

737
00:40:36,850 --> 00:40:38,230
you can do a bisection.

738
00:40:38,230 --> 00:40:40,110
So it's log n to find
the optimal lambda.

739
00:40:40,110 --> 00:40:42,770
So it's pretty nice
because we can really

740
00:40:42,770 --> 00:40:46,490
easily tune the model.

741
00:40:46,490 --> 00:40:48,150
And so what is SIGReg now?

742
00:40:48,150 --> 00:40:51,070
So this is the stuff that avoids
your representation collapse

743
00:40:51,070 --> 00:40:54,500
and make sure that your zt or--

744
00:40:54,500 --> 00:40:59,200
informative about what is
inside your observation.

745
00:40:59,200 --> 00:41:02,500
So basically, you
can see zt as learned

746
00:41:02,500 --> 00:41:05,240
abstraction of the world
or a learned state.

747
00:41:05,240 --> 00:41:08,960
You have an observation of the
world, which is the image ot.

748
00:41:08,960 --> 00:41:12,280
And you try to learn-- you
try to estimate the state,

749
00:41:12,280 --> 00:41:14,060
the ground-truth state.

750
00:41:14,060 --> 00:41:16,680
And so how do you
prevent collapse?

751
00:41:16,680 --> 00:41:19,700
Basically, we use a
simple regularization

752
00:41:19,700 --> 00:41:23,660
called SIGReg or Sketched
Isotropic Gaussian Regularizer

753
00:41:23,660 --> 00:41:28,560
that Randall and Yann LeCun
introduced last November,

754
00:41:28,560 --> 00:41:30,620
I think, if I'm correct.

755
00:41:30,620 --> 00:41:33,060
And the idea is very simple.

756
00:41:33,060 --> 00:41:36,280
The math are a bit tricky, but
I invite you to read the paper.

757
00:41:36,280 --> 00:41:38,340
It's a very nice paper.

758
00:41:38,340 --> 00:41:39,760
But the idea is very simple.

759
00:41:39,760 --> 00:41:42,520
You take the distribution
of your embedding.

760
00:41:42,520 --> 00:41:43,880
So you take your batch.

761
00:41:43,880 --> 00:41:49,020
You look at how your
embedding zt distributed.

762
00:41:49,020 --> 00:41:52,190
And you will try to
optimize that distribution

763
00:41:52,190 --> 00:41:55,350
to be isotropic Gaussian.

764
00:41:55,350 --> 00:41:56,490
So how do you do that?

765
00:41:56,490 --> 00:41:58,110
You could use
generative modeling

766
00:41:58,110 --> 00:42:00,990
to do that with the KL
divergence and everything,

767
00:42:00,990 --> 00:42:04,210
such as Variational Autoencoder.

768
00:42:04,210 --> 00:42:05,450
We don't want that here.

769
00:42:05,450 --> 00:42:07,670
We don't want to use
generative models.

770
00:42:07,670 --> 00:42:10,990
So what you do is
you're going to try

771
00:42:10,990 --> 00:42:13,130
to use a statistical test.

772
00:42:13,130 --> 00:42:15,070
So in statistics,
you have tests that

773
00:42:15,070 --> 00:42:19,230
tells you given an empirical
distribution, how close it--

774
00:42:19,230 --> 00:42:23,590
how close is it to
Gaussian distribution?

775
00:42:23,590 --> 00:42:26,310
The problem is that
our embedding space

776
00:42:26,310 --> 00:42:27,890
is very high-dimensional.

777
00:42:27,890 --> 00:42:30,910
And so there is no test that--

778
00:42:30,910 --> 00:42:33,590
basically, the
statistical test, they

779
00:42:33,590 --> 00:42:35,870
suffer from the curse
of dimensionality,

780
00:42:35,870 --> 00:42:37,910
so it's very difficult
to directly use

781
00:42:37,910 --> 00:42:42,350
a test to optimize in
high-dimensional distribution.

782
00:42:42,350 --> 00:42:45,590
And so what Randall had--

783
00:42:45,590 --> 00:42:50,320
our idea is to
basically project.

784
00:42:50,320 --> 00:42:54,220
You sample a lot of random
direction in your latent space,

785
00:42:54,220 --> 00:42:56,160
and you're going to
project all your embedding

786
00:42:56,160 --> 00:42:58,255
into one dimension.

787
00:42:58,255 --> 00:42:59,880
So as you can see,
for instance, if you

788
00:42:59,880 --> 00:43:06,940
look at the red arrow on this
image and you look on the right,

789
00:43:06,940 --> 00:43:09,600
you can see that once you
project your embedding

790
00:43:09,600 --> 00:43:15,520
into that direction, you get a
unimodal-- sorry, a univariate

791
00:43:15,520 --> 00:43:17,200
empirical distribution.

792
00:43:17,200 --> 00:43:20,900
And now you can optimize because
it's just a 1D distribution.

793
00:43:20,900 --> 00:43:24,720
You can optimize to
make that Gaussian.

794
00:43:24,720 --> 00:43:27,860
And if you do that--
in a lot of directions,

795
00:43:27,860 --> 00:43:30,920
there is a theorem called
Cramér--Wold theorem that says

796
00:43:30,920 --> 00:43:35,160
that if you optimize the
marginals to be Gaussian,

797
00:43:35,160 --> 00:43:36,940
then the joint is
going to be Gaussian.

798
00:43:36,940 --> 00:43:39,700
So basically, if you do that
for a lot of random direction,

799
00:43:39,700 --> 00:43:42,960
you can prove that your latent
embedding distribution is

800
00:43:42,960 --> 00:43:45,160
going to be Gaussian as well.

801
00:43:45,160 --> 00:43:51,420
So it makes us sure that we
will have informative embedding.

802
00:43:51,420 --> 00:43:53,220
So that's pretty nice.

803
00:43:53,220 --> 00:43:56,000
OK, so now how can
you-- let's imagine

804
00:43:56,000 --> 00:43:58,440
you train your world model,
blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

805
00:43:58,440 --> 00:44:00,980
How can you make sure that
you learn a good world model?

806
00:44:00,980 --> 00:44:02,360
So there is two ways.

807
00:44:02,360 --> 00:44:03,980
I mean, I know two ways.

808
00:44:03,980 --> 00:44:05,420
If you more than, that's nice.

809
00:44:05,420 --> 00:44:06,620
We can talk about that.

810
00:44:06,620 --> 00:44:09,620
But the first way
is online control.

811
00:44:09,620 --> 00:44:13,360
So can you use your LeWorldModel
that is action-conditioned?

812
00:44:13,360 --> 00:44:16,560
And can you in an online
way optimize this action

813
00:44:16,560 --> 00:44:18,200
to perform control?

814
00:44:18,200 --> 00:44:20,080
So this is the first thing.

815
00:44:20,080 --> 00:44:22,920
And the second thing we are
going to talk about after is

816
00:44:22,920 --> 00:44:26,400
try to assess if your
world model understand

817
00:44:26,400 --> 00:44:27,840
intuitive physics.

818
00:44:27,840 --> 00:44:30,040
But let's focus on
control for now.

819
00:44:30,040 --> 00:44:33,600
So how we perform control is
basically we are going to--

820
00:44:33,600 --> 00:44:36,840
with your model,
the LeWorldModel,

821
00:44:36,840 --> 00:44:41,800
you're going to sample random
trajectories of action.

822
00:44:41,800 --> 00:44:46,170
And you're going to optimize
by rolling out the future.

823
00:44:46,170 --> 00:44:50,690
You will optimize that
sequence of action

824
00:44:50,690 --> 00:44:53,010
to match a target goal.

825
00:44:53,010 --> 00:44:55,930
So for instance, let's say
you have a goal frame Og.

826
00:44:55,930 --> 00:44:58,170
You start from the
current frame O1.

827
00:44:58,170 --> 00:45:00,790
You both encode them
through your encoder.

828
00:45:00,790 --> 00:45:04,890
You got the initial
state, latent state Z1.

829
00:45:04,890 --> 00:45:09,130
You have your target
state, which is zg.

830
00:45:09,130 --> 00:45:12,550
And then you sample an
initial sequence of action.

831
00:45:12,550 --> 00:45:16,010
And you use the first
one with your first state

832
00:45:16,010 --> 00:45:16,970
inside the predictor.

833
00:45:16,970 --> 00:45:18,105
It gives you z2.

834
00:45:18,105 --> 00:45:20,730
Then you use the second one, et
cetera, blah, blah, blah, blah,

835
00:45:20,730 --> 00:45:21,230
blah.

836
00:45:21,230 --> 00:45:23,170
And after each state--

837
00:45:23,170 --> 00:45:26,330
each step, sorry,
you compare how far

838
00:45:26,330 --> 00:45:30,530
are you in your latent space to
the representation of the goal.

839
00:45:30,530 --> 00:45:32,890
It can be MSE.

840
00:45:32,890 --> 00:45:35,090
For instance, you
use the MSE loss

841
00:45:35,090 --> 00:45:37,290
to estimate how far
you are from your goal.

842
00:45:37,290 --> 00:45:39,990
And because your predictor
is differentiable,

843
00:45:39,990 --> 00:45:42,650
you can for instance
backpropagate

844
00:45:42,650 --> 00:45:45,500
until the action try to--

845
00:45:45,500 --> 00:45:50,940
sequence of action to minimize
the distance with your goal.

846
00:45:50,940 --> 00:45:53,700
So I can show you some results.

847
00:45:53,700 --> 00:45:57,520
You can see that we
consider four tasks.

848
00:45:57,520 --> 00:46:00,240
So the first one on the
left is called TwoRoom.

849
00:46:00,240 --> 00:46:03,860
It's a simple 2D navigation task
where you need to move from one

850
00:46:03,860 --> 00:46:07,240
side of a room into the other
by passing through a door.

851
00:46:07,240 --> 00:46:09,040
Then you have Reacher.

852
00:46:09,040 --> 00:46:12,780
It comes from DeepMind Control
Suite, where you need to--

853
00:46:12,780 --> 00:46:17,700
you can see on the right
you have a 2D like--

854
00:46:17,700 --> 00:46:19,380
sorry, a two-joint arm.

855
00:46:19,380 --> 00:46:23,360
And you need to match a target
location from a given goal.

856
00:46:23,360 --> 00:46:26,300
Then you have Push-T.

857
00:46:26,300 --> 00:46:29,080
So Push-T is already present.

858
00:46:29,080 --> 00:46:33,780
But the goal of Push-T is
to push basically the T

859
00:46:33,780 --> 00:46:35,038
to the green area.

860
00:46:35,038 --> 00:46:36,080
That's as simple as that.

861
00:46:36,080 --> 00:46:37,040
And you can only push.

862
00:46:37,040 --> 00:46:38,500
You cannot pull.

863
00:46:38,500 --> 00:46:43,190
And OG-Bench Cube, it's a 3D
environment where the objective

864
00:46:43,190 --> 00:46:46,590
of that environment
is to make the cube--

865
00:46:46,590 --> 00:46:49,230
your cube, it's a 3D
manipulation robotic arm where

866
00:46:49,230 --> 00:46:52,630
you need to make the cube
matching a target location

867
00:46:52,630 --> 00:46:53,830
as well.

868
00:46:53,830 --> 00:46:57,910
And so I think the most
interesting result is--

869
00:46:57,910 --> 00:47:00,550
the first one is
Push-T, because you

870
00:47:00,550 --> 00:47:04,150
can see that DINO
World Model has been

871
00:47:04,150 --> 00:47:05,890
trained with proprioception.

872
00:47:05,890 --> 00:47:07,470
And you can see
that LeWorldModel

873
00:47:07,470 --> 00:47:11,230
without proprioception beat DINO
World Model with proprioception

874
00:47:11,230 --> 00:47:14,390
with half the parameter.

875
00:47:14,390 --> 00:47:16,510
But what is most
interesting is if you remove

876
00:47:16,510 --> 00:47:18,530
the proprioception
of DINO World Model,

877
00:47:18,530 --> 00:47:21,130
you see that the
performance drop.

878
00:47:21,130 --> 00:47:24,090
And LeWorldModel, because it
doesn't have proprioception,

879
00:47:24,090 --> 00:47:26,430
it beats by a lot
the LeWorldModel

880
00:47:26,430 --> 00:47:27,830
without proprioception.

881
00:47:27,830 --> 00:47:30,550
And PLDM, which is
the only other one

882
00:47:30,550 --> 00:47:33,550
that doesn't use proprioception
and that is fully trained end

883
00:47:33,550 --> 00:47:36,510
to end, also have very bad
performance on Push-T. So

884
00:47:36,510 --> 00:47:39,710
it was very nice
to see this result.

885
00:47:39,710 --> 00:47:43,440
So for Reacher, we beat PLDM
and DINO World Model as well.

886
00:47:43,440 --> 00:47:46,080
But what is really interesting
is that for OG-Bench Cube,

887
00:47:46,080 --> 00:47:51,320
for instance, we beat PLDM, but
we don't beat DINO World Model.

888
00:47:51,320 --> 00:47:54,520
And the most likely
explanation for that

889
00:47:54,520 --> 00:47:57,560
is that we only
train on trajectories

890
00:47:57,560 --> 00:47:59,680
coming from the data sets.

891
00:47:59,680 --> 00:48:02,920
But DINO World Model has
been pretrained because it's

892
00:48:02,920 --> 00:48:04,060
a pretrained encoder.

893
00:48:04,060 --> 00:48:08,280
It has been pretrained on
124 million natural images.

894
00:48:08,280 --> 00:48:11,920
And consequently, it has a
better understanding about

895
00:48:11,920 --> 00:48:16,560
objects and 3D than we have
because it has been trained

896
00:48:16,560 --> 00:48:20,000
on a lot, lot, lot more data.

897
00:48:20,000 --> 00:48:22,780
And also very funnily, you
can see that for TwoRoom,

898
00:48:22,780 --> 00:48:28,520
even if it's the simplest
task, almost all the baseline

899
00:48:28,520 --> 00:48:30,060
destroy the task.

900
00:48:30,060 --> 00:48:31,380
But for us, we don't.

901
00:48:31,380 --> 00:48:34,100
And this is actually
a limitation of SIGReg

902
00:48:34,100 --> 00:48:38,620
that exists for now is f if you
have intrinsic dimensionality.

903
00:48:38,620 --> 00:48:42,320
So what I mean by that is the
true dimensionality that you

904
00:48:42,320 --> 00:48:43,580
need to solve the problem.

905
00:48:43,580 --> 00:48:45,620
For instance, so
TwoRoom is just 2,

906
00:48:45,620 --> 00:48:48,123
because you just need to
the x and y of the agent.

907
00:48:48,123 --> 00:48:50,540
As soon as you know that and
I give you a target location,

908
00:48:50,540 --> 00:48:52,680
you can solve the environment.

909
00:48:52,680 --> 00:48:56,340
If that is very much smaller
than the embedding you use,

910
00:48:56,340 --> 00:48:59,820
there is no way that you can
produce Gaussian embeddings.

911
00:48:59,820 --> 00:49:03,280
At least your SIGReg
when you optimize

912
00:49:03,280 --> 00:49:08,080
is going to need to create fake
stuff to be able to be Gaussian,

913
00:49:08,080 --> 00:49:11,520
fake, like to use some
information to make your latent

914
00:49:11,520 --> 00:49:12,880
space Gaussian.

915
00:49:12,880 --> 00:49:15,880
So it doesn't help
you, basically.

916
00:49:15,880 --> 00:49:18,060
There is research going
on to try to fix that.

917
00:49:18,060 --> 00:49:20,840
And actually, we know
that if you carefully

918
00:49:20,840 --> 00:49:24,960
tune the hyperparameter, you can
somewhat overcome this issue.

919
00:49:24,960 --> 00:49:29,080
But for the sake of
fairness, we didn't hypertune

920
00:49:29,080 --> 00:49:31,920
all the hyperparameters, and
we keep the same hyperparameter

921
00:49:31,920 --> 00:49:34,680
across all the environment.

922
00:49:34,680 --> 00:49:37,400
But the most interesting
part why it's very nice

923
00:49:37,400 --> 00:49:39,650
is when you look at
the planning time.

924
00:49:39,650 --> 00:49:42,050
So for DINO World
Model-- and we heavily

925
00:49:42,050 --> 00:49:46,490
optimized DINO World Model to
try to be as fast as possible.

926
00:49:46,490 --> 00:49:51,750
The fastest we could go
for doing the full planning

927
00:49:51,750 --> 00:49:54,470
is 47 seconds.

928
00:49:54,470 --> 00:49:58,210
And the reason for that
is because you need to--

929
00:49:58,210 --> 00:50:00,763
your predictor takes
all the patches.

930
00:50:00,763 --> 00:50:02,430
And you need to predict
all the patches,

931
00:50:02,430 --> 00:50:05,090
so it's quite slow because
of the quadratic cost

932
00:50:05,090 --> 00:50:09,470
of the attention to
predict the future.

933
00:50:09,470 --> 00:50:12,610
As for us, with LeWorldModel,
we have a single embedding

934
00:50:12,610 --> 00:50:15,210
for representing
the latent state

935
00:50:15,210 --> 00:50:18,370
because we just use the
CLS token of the encoder.

936
00:50:18,370 --> 00:50:21,550
And it allows us
some other tricks.

937
00:50:21,550 --> 00:50:23,430
We didn't optimize
a lot for that.

938
00:50:23,430 --> 00:50:26,430
We could go a lot
less than this.

939
00:50:26,430 --> 00:50:28,650
But we can go to a
full playing time

940
00:50:28,650 --> 00:50:30,630
under the second,
which is very nice.

941
00:50:30,630 --> 00:50:35,010
It's almost 50 times faster.

942
00:50:35,010 --> 00:50:39,100
Another thing interesting is
that if you fix the FLOP--

943
00:50:39,100 --> 00:50:42,100
so if you fix the FLOP that
you used for planning--

944
00:50:42,100 --> 00:50:45,200
and we fix the FLOP at
those of LeWorldModel.

945
00:50:45,200 --> 00:50:47,180
So if you reduce
the planning time

946
00:50:47,180 --> 00:50:49,860
by tweaking the hyperparameter
for the planning

947
00:50:49,860 --> 00:50:53,460
until DINO World Model
plan under a second,

948
00:50:53,460 --> 00:50:58,180
you can see that the success
rate drop by a lot for Push-T

949
00:50:58,180 --> 00:51:00,880
and for OG-Bench Cube,
which is expected.

950
00:51:00,880 --> 00:51:04,580
But it showed that
at similar budget,

951
00:51:04,580 --> 00:51:12,260
we outperform even on OG-Bench
Cube DINO World Model.

952
00:51:12,260 --> 00:51:13,420
So that's for the control.

953
00:51:13,420 --> 00:51:15,120
For intuitive physics
understanding,

954
00:51:15,120 --> 00:51:18,820
we did something pretty
similar-- pretty easy, sorry.

955
00:51:18,820 --> 00:51:21,760
The first thing is we
probe the latent space.

956
00:51:21,760 --> 00:51:25,940
So we just took the encoder from
LeWorldModel, and we froze it.

957
00:51:25,940 --> 00:51:29,980
And we train a linear or
NLP probe, non-linear probe,

958
00:51:29,980 --> 00:51:34,710
to try to predict the
coefficients of the simulation

959
00:51:34,710 --> 00:51:36,350
for that state.

960
00:51:36,350 --> 00:51:39,220
So I will not go that
much into the detail.

961
00:51:39,220 --> 00:51:40,970
You can have a look
at the paper for that.

962
00:51:40,970 --> 00:51:43,730
But what is pretty interesting
is that for instance,

963
00:51:43,730 --> 00:51:47,350
for OG-Bench Cube, you can
see that the linear probe with

964
00:51:47,350 --> 00:51:50,830
LeWorldModel, we almost all the
time have a lower mean squared

965
00:51:50,830 --> 00:51:55,030
error, which means that--

966
00:51:55,030 --> 00:51:58,310
if you compare with the
NLP probe on the right,

967
00:51:58,310 --> 00:52:00,230
it's almost DINO World
Model all the time

968
00:52:00,230 --> 00:52:04,350
that have the lowest
mean squared error.

969
00:52:04,350 --> 00:52:08,910
And so what it suggests
is that our latent space

970
00:52:08,910 --> 00:52:12,730
is less entangled than
one on DINO World Model.

971
00:52:12,730 --> 00:52:16,310
So it's more easy to recover
the coefficient directly

972
00:52:16,310 --> 00:52:19,752
from the latent space rather
than DINO World Model.

973
00:52:19,752 --> 00:52:21,210
And it doesn't make
sense, I think,

974
00:52:21,210 --> 00:52:24,310
because as we used SIGReg,
it pushed like the latent

975
00:52:24,310 --> 00:52:27,510
space in such a way
where each dimension are

976
00:52:27,510 --> 00:52:28,690
somewhat meaningless.

977
00:52:28,690 --> 00:52:30,670
So it's not fully
disentangled, but it's

978
00:52:30,670 --> 00:52:35,280
somewhat more than Dino World
Model, which is pretty nice.

979
00:52:35,280 --> 00:52:37,740
Another thing that you could
do, which is pretty cool,

980
00:52:37,740 --> 00:52:41,480
is to try to see what happens
when you violate the world

981
00:52:41,480 --> 00:52:41,980
model.

982
00:52:41,980 --> 00:52:45,700
So if a sudden change
in the dynamics happen,

983
00:52:45,700 --> 00:52:50,320
does your world model predict
that this is a violation?

984
00:52:50,320 --> 00:52:52,440
And so what we did
for that is, as you

985
00:52:52,440 --> 00:52:55,000
can see on the left-- sorry,
the cube color and cube

986
00:52:55,000 --> 00:52:57,000
teleportation are inverted.

987
00:52:57,000 --> 00:52:58,560
But if you look on
the left, you can

988
00:52:58,560 --> 00:53:01,840
see that we have a
normal trajectory where

989
00:53:01,840 --> 00:53:05,240
your robotic arm pick up the
cube and move it to the right,

990
00:53:05,240 --> 00:53:07,440
for instance, OK?

991
00:53:07,440 --> 00:53:09,080
Nothing happened.

992
00:53:09,080 --> 00:53:10,980
And then we consider
two perturbation.

993
00:53:10,980 --> 00:53:13,300
For instance, we change
the color of the cube.

994
00:53:13,300 --> 00:53:15,880
So this is the one on the
right, not in the middle.

995
00:53:15,880 --> 00:53:18,760
Suddenly, at a given frame, OK?

996
00:53:18,760 --> 00:53:22,960
And another transformation we
did is randomly, like suddenly--

997
00:53:22,960 --> 00:53:27,200
this is the one in the
middle-- the cube teleport.

998
00:53:27,200 --> 00:53:30,800
And so if you look
on the right, you

999
00:53:30,800 --> 00:53:34,680
can see that the x-axis
are the time steps, OK?

1000
00:53:34,680 --> 00:53:37,180
And the y-axis is
the prediction error.

1001
00:53:37,180 --> 00:53:39,600
So this is the
difference between what

1002
00:53:39,600 --> 00:53:43,720
your predictor predict
is going to happen

1003
00:53:43,720 --> 00:53:48,120
and what the actual embedding of
the next states look like, OK?

1004
00:53:48,120 --> 00:53:50,320
And so what you can
see is that if you

1005
00:53:50,320 --> 00:53:53,340
don't have any perturbation--
so this is the gray line--

1006
00:53:53,340 --> 00:53:54,220
it's fine.

1007
00:53:54,220 --> 00:53:57,480
If you change the cube
color, it's a bit higher,

1008
00:53:57,480 --> 00:53:58,980
but it's very negligible.

1009
00:53:58,980 --> 00:54:01,680
So it means that basically your
world model don't care much

1010
00:54:01,680 --> 00:54:03,360
about the color
of the cube, which

1011
00:54:03,360 --> 00:54:06,640
is pretty cool because you don't
need that for the dynamics.

1012
00:54:06,640 --> 00:54:09,000
You don't care about
the color of the cube.

1013
00:54:09,000 --> 00:54:11,380
But if suddenly
the cube teleports,

1014
00:54:11,380 --> 00:54:15,140
then the prediction
error shoot a lot,

1015
00:54:15,140 --> 00:54:18,480
meaning that your world
model didn't predict that.

1016
00:54:18,480 --> 00:54:20,940
Some people say to
me often that yeah,

1017
00:54:20,940 --> 00:54:24,137
but it's just
out-of-distribution.

1018
00:54:24,137 --> 00:54:25,720
And I would say it's
true, but I think

1019
00:54:25,720 --> 00:54:29,200
it's not very meaningful to
say that because as human, when

1020
00:54:29,200 --> 00:54:32,310
you violate your model, it's
also very out-of-distribution.

1021
00:54:32,310 --> 00:54:34,490
That's why, for
instance, when someone

1022
00:54:34,490 --> 00:54:37,410
do to you a magic trick where
they make a coin disappear

1023
00:54:37,410 --> 00:54:39,450
in front of you, you're
suddenly very surprised,

1024
00:54:39,450 --> 00:54:41,230
and it frustrates you a bit.

1025
00:54:41,230 --> 00:54:42,610
It's the same here.

1026
00:54:42,610 --> 00:54:46,530
It's because the prediction
error is very high.

1027
00:54:46,530 --> 00:54:49,750
So we did two other small
experiments as well.

1028
00:54:49,750 --> 00:54:55,090
The first one is we try to
embed the location of the agent

1029
00:54:55,090 --> 00:54:58,970
and the T. And we try to make
the agent location and the T

1030
00:54:58,970 --> 00:55:00,250
location move.

1031
00:55:00,250 --> 00:55:05,110
So you can see that the middle
plot shows you what happened,

1032
00:55:05,110 --> 00:55:07,030
the different location.

1033
00:55:07,030 --> 00:55:10,350
And we try to project
with a t-SNE plot

1034
00:55:10,350 --> 00:55:14,930
the embedding space to try
to see if we can recover

1035
00:55:14,930 --> 00:55:17,850
this exact relative
distance between all

1036
00:55:17,850 --> 00:55:20,450
the different locations
of the original space.

1037
00:55:20,450 --> 00:55:23,010
And what you can see is
that up to a permutation

1038
00:55:23,010 --> 00:55:27,790
of the axis and reflection,
this is exactly what happened.

1039
00:55:27,790 --> 00:55:31,380
So we recover basically
the relative distance

1040
00:55:31,380 --> 00:55:34,820
in the original space,
which is pretty nice.

1041
00:55:34,820 --> 00:55:38,700
The last thing we did is
we froze the world model,

1042
00:55:38,700 --> 00:55:42,860
and we train a decoder to try to
interpret what is happening when

1043
00:55:42,860 --> 00:55:44,320
you make future prediction.

1044
00:55:44,320 --> 00:55:48,928
And so for that, you can
see that we give a context

1045
00:55:48,928 --> 00:55:49,720
to our world model.

1046
00:55:49,720 --> 00:55:52,460
So this is the first three
frame of the top row you see,

1047
00:55:52,460 --> 00:55:55,160
or the bottom, the
second row, OK?

1048
00:55:55,160 --> 00:55:57,100
So we give that to our model.

1049
00:55:57,100 --> 00:56:00,020
The first row is really
what happened in the future,

1050
00:56:00,020 --> 00:56:02,360
on the right, where you
have open-loop prediction.

1051
00:56:02,360 --> 00:56:05,180
So this is actually what
happened in the trajectory.

1052
00:56:05,180 --> 00:56:08,560
And then on the second row,
you have what your world model

1053
00:56:08,560 --> 00:56:11,420
imagine when you give the
same sequence of action that

1054
00:56:11,420 --> 00:56:13,760
has been taken for
the original sequence.

1055
00:56:13,760 --> 00:56:16,780
And you can see that
the world model predicts

1056
00:56:16,780 --> 00:56:22,108
somewhat the reality when you
give the sequence of action.

1057
00:56:22,108 --> 00:56:23,900
But what is very
interesting is that if you

1058
00:56:23,900 --> 00:56:28,650
look at the second
rollout with the cube--

1059
00:56:28,650 --> 00:56:33,270
so you can see that we
predict very well what is

1060
00:56:33,270 --> 00:56:34,710
going to happen with the cube.

1061
00:56:34,710 --> 00:56:38,790
But if you are very careful, you
can see that at frame 15 and 20,

1062
00:56:38,790 --> 00:56:42,990
the angle of the
gripper is opposite.

1063
00:56:42,990 --> 00:56:45,550
And so basically, you can see
that the world model didn't

1064
00:56:45,550 --> 00:56:48,510
learn the rotation
of the gripper, which

1065
00:56:48,510 --> 00:56:52,310
was pretty interesting, because
it still was able to solve

1066
00:56:52,310 --> 00:56:55,750
somewhat the environment.

1067
00:56:55,750 --> 00:56:57,670
So there is many
limitations for our model,

1068
00:56:57,670 --> 00:57:00,390
or as I like to call them,
research opportunities.

1069
00:57:00,390 --> 00:57:05,250
So for now, you are doomed to
short-term planning horizon.

1070
00:57:05,250 --> 00:57:08,550
So if you can unlock that,
that would be very nice.

1071
00:57:08,550 --> 00:57:13,270
Another problem is that you have
them at a single temporal level.

1072
00:57:13,270 --> 00:57:15,430
So we need
hierarchies, basically.

1073
00:57:15,430 --> 00:57:17,190
For instance, when
you think about oh, I

1074
00:57:17,190 --> 00:57:21,630
need to go to the airport, you
think at a different hierarchy.

1075
00:57:21,630 --> 00:57:23,090
The airport is your goal.

1076
00:57:23,090 --> 00:57:25,620
You think, OK, I
need to go to my car,

1077
00:57:25,620 --> 00:57:28,280
then I need to go to airport
and blah, blah, blah.

1078
00:57:28,280 --> 00:57:32,400
And then only at the end, it
translates into muscle movement.

1079
00:57:32,400 --> 00:57:35,340
So you don't all the time think
in terms of muscle movement.

1080
00:57:35,340 --> 00:57:38,640
So we need that as well to
be able to predict further

1081
00:57:38,640 --> 00:57:40,160
in the future.

1082
00:57:40,160 --> 00:57:41,640
A very important
stuff as well is

1083
00:57:41,640 --> 00:57:44,940
to move from this
toy environment.

1084
00:57:44,940 --> 00:57:48,540
I fully agree with
this kind of criticism,

1085
00:57:48,540 --> 00:57:51,140
that it's very
toy-ish experiments.

1086
00:57:51,140 --> 00:57:53,640
And so can we move to
real-world robotics

1087
00:57:53,640 --> 00:57:56,200
or very stochastic and
partially observable

1088
00:57:56,200 --> 00:57:57,520
environment like Minecraft.

1089
00:57:57,520 --> 00:57:59,320
That would be very nice.

1090
00:57:59,320 --> 00:58:01,080
And also, a big
problem I think is,

1091
00:58:01,080 --> 00:58:02,660
how do you specify your goal?

1092
00:58:02,660 --> 00:58:04,900
So for now, you can see,
as I explained before,

1093
00:58:04,900 --> 00:58:08,000
you need to provide a visual
goal, but you don't all the time

1094
00:58:08,000 --> 00:58:09,020
have access to that.

1095
00:58:09,020 --> 00:58:10,840
And it doesn't tell
you anything about how

1096
00:58:10,840 --> 00:58:12,060
you should solve the task.

1097
00:58:12,060 --> 00:58:16,680
So for instance, if you have
a plane that needs to land,

1098
00:58:16,680 --> 00:58:19,360
you don't want to just show
a picture of the plane landed

1099
00:58:19,360 --> 00:58:20,300
to do the planning.

1100
00:58:20,300 --> 00:58:23,600
You want to specify
how smooth should

1101
00:58:23,600 --> 00:58:25,060
be the landing and everything.

1102
00:58:25,060 --> 00:58:30,280
So we don't how to do that for
now with this kind of approach.

1103
00:58:30,280 --> 00:58:32,040
I would like also to
take just two minutes

1104
00:58:32,040 --> 00:58:35,880
to do a bit of advertisement for
something we have been pushing

1105
00:58:35,880 --> 00:58:39,040
with a lot of Randall's
students and a lot of people

1106
00:58:39,040 --> 00:58:42,920
for the past few months, which
is called stable-worldmodel.

1107
00:58:42,920 --> 00:58:44,800
And if you're interested
about world model

1108
00:58:44,800 --> 00:58:47,960
research and this kind of
stuff, you should definitely

1109
00:58:47,960 --> 00:58:48,940
have a look for that.

1110
00:58:48,940 --> 00:58:52,320
So it's a GitHub library,
fully open-source,

1111
00:58:52,320 --> 00:58:54,940
that allows you to train
very easily world models.

1112
00:58:54,940 --> 00:58:57,400
So you have all the baseline
I discussed about and Hazel

1113
00:58:57,400 --> 00:59:01,080
discuss about that are
implemented there and heavily

1114
00:59:01,080 --> 00:59:01,580
tested.

1115
00:59:01,580 --> 00:59:03,840
You have all solver
to do planning.

1116
00:59:03,840 --> 00:59:05,380
You have many environments.

1117
00:59:05,380 --> 00:59:09,000
We recently added DeepMind
Control and Minecraft.

1118
00:59:09,000 --> 00:59:12,840
And we are in discussion to add
support for real robot data also

1119
00:59:12,840 --> 00:59:13,960
very soon.

1120
00:59:13,960 --> 00:59:16,320
And everything is
very heavily tested,

1121
00:59:16,320 --> 00:59:18,640
and there is documentation.

1122
00:59:18,640 --> 00:59:21,650
So yeah, feel free to give a try
and give feedback or contribute

1123
00:59:21,650 --> 00:59:22,230
to library.

1124
00:59:22,230 --> 00:59:24,730
That would be very cool.

1125
00:59:24,730 --> 00:59:27,290
Thank you.
1126
00:59:27,290 --> 00:59:28,030
OK, great.

1127
00:59:28,030 --> 00:59:29,450
Thank you so much.

1128
00:59:29,450 --> 00:59:32,630
Let's give a round of applause
to our speakers today.

1129
00:59:32,630 --> 00:59:36,112
[APPLAUSE]

1130
00:59:37,610 --> 00:59:40,110
So now we'll be
soliciting some questions.

1131
00:59:40,110 --> 00:59:43,290
We have some online on
Slido, but I'll also

1132
00:59:43,290 --> 00:59:46,610
be looking for in-person
questions in case anybody

1133
00:59:46,610 --> 00:59:49,130
here wants to ask
anything as well.

1134
00:59:49,130 --> 00:59:51,450
So we'll balance between both.

1135
00:59:51,450 --> 00:59:55,870
And I'll let you guys figure out
who should answer each question.

1136
00:59:55,870 --> 01:00:01,970
Or if you both have insights
for anything, that's also great.

1137
01:00:01,970 --> 01:00:05,423
Yeah, does anybody here
have any questions?

1138
01:00:05,423 --> 01:00:09,170
Well, I'm just curious
how does the world model

1139
01:00:09,170 --> 01:00:10,950
transfer to physical AI space?

1140
01:00:10,950 --> 01:00:15,770
How can we see them moving
forward in robotic space?

1141
01:00:15,770 --> 01:00:17,770
I would give it to
Lucas, probably.

1142
01:00:17,770 --> 01:00:20,060
Lucas, can you hear us?

1143
01:00:20,060 --> 01:00:20,680
Yes, yes.

1144
01:00:20,680 --> 01:00:22,460
Can you repeat the
question, please?

1145
01:00:22,460 --> 01:00:23,660
Oh.

1146
01:00:23,660 --> 01:00:28,460
So how can we expect the world
models in the physical AI space?

1147
01:00:28,460 --> 01:00:31,140
Because we already have a
diffusion-based foundation

1148
01:00:31,140 --> 01:00:35,740
models like GR00T or maybe
pi, and also Psi-Zero.

1149
01:00:35,740 --> 01:00:38,660
Using world models, can we
expect some more applications

1150
01:00:38,660 --> 01:00:41,220
in the robotics space?

1151
01:00:41,220 --> 01:00:42,500
OK, OK, OK.

1152
01:00:42,500 --> 01:00:46,180
So first of all, I
will be very skeptical

1153
01:00:46,180 --> 01:00:48,820
that the current VLA models
have a good understanding

1154
01:00:48,820 --> 01:00:49,600
of the world.

1155
01:00:49,600 --> 01:00:51,160
There is no reason for that.

1156
01:00:51,160 --> 01:00:55,740
They are not trained to predict
the consequence of their action,

1157
01:00:55,740 --> 01:00:56,420
OK?

1158
01:00:56,420 --> 01:01:00,780
And I will be very
skeptical to see something

1159
01:01:00,780 --> 01:01:03,360
that doesn't know what is
the outcome of his action,

1160
01:01:03,360 --> 01:01:06,460
be very reliable
to the physical AI.

1161
01:01:06,460 --> 01:01:09,980
So as human, why you are
very good at what you do

1162
01:01:09,980 --> 01:01:12,620
is because you can
predict what is

1163
01:01:12,620 --> 01:01:15,340
the consequence of your
action in the real world.

1164
01:01:15,340 --> 01:01:17,860
And that's what world
model try to do.

1165
01:01:17,860 --> 01:01:19,130
VLA don't do that.

1166
01:01:19,130 --> 01:01:21,590
So if you want to
have physical AI,

1167
01:01:21,590 --> 01:01:23,030
basically, you need world model.

1168
01:01:23,030 --> 01:01:24,690
You cannot bypass that.

1169
01:01:24,690 --> 01:01:29,730
I think, as Yann say, often
like VLA and everything,

1170
01:01:29,730 --> 01:01:31,950
they can be very
helpful for simple stuff

1171
01:01:31,950 --> 01:01:33,670
where you don't
need to predict what

1172
01:01:33,670 --> 01:01:37,030
is going to be the outcome of
your action in the real world.

1173
01:01:37,030 --> 01:01:40,770
For instance, if you want
to make robots that--

1174
01:01:40,770 --> 01:01:44,150
I don't know-- do some dance
or whatever, they only need

1175
01:01:44,150 --> 01:01:46,290
to their internal dynamics, OK?

1176
01:01:46,290 --> 01:01:48,850
They don't need how to
interact with the real world.

1177
01:01:48,850 --> 01:01:50,350
To interact with
the real world, you

1178
01:01:50,350 --> 01:01:52,170
need to predict the
outcome of your action,

1179
01:01:52,170 --> 01:01:53,290
so you need world models.

1180
01:01:53,290 --> 01:01:54,730
I hope that answers
your question.

1181
01:01:54,730 --> 01:01:56,630
If not, happy to clarify.

1182
01:01:56,630 --> 01:01:57,290
Sure, sure.

1183
01:01:57,290 --> 01:01:58,470
Thank you.

1184
01:01:58,470 --> 01:02:01,790
I would like to answer
one more sentence.

1185
01:02:01,790 --> 01:02:04,190
I think Professor
Sherry Yang in NYU,

1186
01:02:04,190 --> 01:02:07,310
she has an opinion
that world model can

1187
01:02:07,310 --> 01:02:09,870
be an evaluator for policies.

1188
01:02:09,870 --> 01:02:11,650
So I think this can
be also your answer.

1189
01:02:11,650 --> 01:02:12,150
Yeah.

1190
01:02:15,310 --> 01:02:17,420
We have someone at Slido.

1191
01:02:17,420 --> 01:02:18,560
Let me see.

1192
01:02:18,560 --> 01:02:22,540
Someone's asking, masking is
shown to improve predictions,

1193
01:02:22,540 --> 01:02:25,180
but is it really necessary
to learn the world model?

1194
01:02:25,180 --> 01:02:29,520
Does it mean that prediction
loss alone is insufficient?

1195
01:02:29,520 --> 01:02:32,623
The prediction loss--
OK, let me assume

1196
01:02:32,623 --> 01:02:34,040
that you're talking
about-- you're

1197
01:02:34,040 --> 01:02:37,440
still talking about the
object-centric representation.

1198
01:02:37,440 --> 01:02:39,040
If you only do the
prediction loss

1199
01:02:39,040 --> 01:02:42,040
with no object-centric
representation, the shortcut

1200
01:02:42,040 --> 01:02:46,772
or what model we learn is
it can be the self-dynamics.

1201
01:02:46,772 --> 01:02:48,480
We cannot guarantee
that the model learns

1202
01:02:48,480 --> 01:02:51,240
the interaction-based dynamics.

1203
01:02:51,240 --> 01:02:56,640
So I won't say that
it's insufficient,

1204
01:02:56,640 --> 01:03:00,640
but with the masking, I think
you can reinforce the model

1205
01:03:00,640 --> 01:03:04,840
to learn the object dynamics.

1206
01:03:04,840 --> 01:03:08,360
Yeah, that makes sense.

1207
01:03:08,360 --> 01:03:10,320
Someone's asking, do
you have any insights

1208
01:03:10,320 --> 01:03:13,400
on how C-JEPA
learns to plan ahead

1209
01:03:13,400 --> 01:03:15,470
beyond just the next frame?

1210
01:03:19,570 --> 01:03:23,630
So for the-- is it
all of the question?

1211
01:03:23,630 --> 01:03:24,130
Yeah.

1212
01:03:24,130 --> 01:03:25,170
OK.

1213
01:03:25,170 --> 01:03:27,790
We strictly follow
the evaluation method.

1214
01:03:27,790 --> 01:03:30,210
I mean, the planning of
the DINO World Model.

1215
01:03:30,210 --> 01:03:32,690
So with the predicted
future frame,

1216
01:03:32,690 --> 01:03:36,610
we do with autoregressively to
reach the long-horizon planning.

1217
01:03:36,610 --> 01:03:41,830
And we use the same, the
parameters for planning as well.

1218
01:03:44,930 --> 01:03:46,790
Any in-person questions?

1219
01:03:49,490 --> 01:03:51,890
OK, I'll continue
with the online ones

1220
01:03:51,890 --> 01:03:53,810
because we have quite a lot.

1221
01:03:53,810 --> 01:03:54,790
Let me see.

1222
01:03:58,050 --> 01:04:01,050
Someone's asking, what would a
JEPA native agent architecture

1223
01:04:01,050 --> 01:04:05,610
look like compared to
current LLM-based agents?

1224
01:04:05,610 --> 01:04:09,050
Lucas, do you want to answer?

1225
01:04:09,050 --> 01:04:09,550
Of course.

1226
01:04:09,550 --> 01:04:10,925
Can you just repeat
the question?

1227
01:04:10,925 --> 01:04:13,170
It's pretty difficult to hear.

1228
01:04:13,170 --> 01:04:16,450
Someone asked, what would a
JEPA native agent architecture

1229
01:04:16,450 --> 01:04:22,690
look like compared to
current LLM-based agents?

1230
01:04:22,690 --> 01:04:24,370
OK.

1231
01:04:24,370 --> 01:04:27,090
Basically, the way it
differs is that you

1232
01:04:27,090 --> 01:04:28,930
learn action-conditioned
model, right?

1233
01:04:28,930 --> 01:04:32,582
So LLM agent, you don't learn
action-conditioned model,

1234
01:04:32,582 --> 01:04:33,290
as far as I know.

1235
01:04:33,290 --> 01:04:36,170
I'm not expert in LLM as well.

1236
01:04:36,170 --> 01:04:39,890
But you learn to use tooling
and to make tool calls.

1237
01:04:39,890 --> 01:04:43,770
This is not what you do
when you do world models.

1238
01:04:43,770 --> 01:04:46,190
What you do is you learn,
OK, I have an action.

1239
01:04:46,190 --> 01:04:47,110
I am a current state.

1240
01:04:47,110 --> 01:04:49,610
What is going to happen in the
future if I take that action?

1241
01:04:49,610 --> 01:04:51,630
This is not how you train LLMs.

1242
01:04:51,630 --> 01:04:53,730
So this is one
step that differs.

1243
01:04:53,730 --> 01:04:57,370
And the second thing is that
by construction, you can just

1244
01:04:57,370 --> 01:05:01,730
learn to model the
future with world models

1245
01:05:01,730 --> 01:05:05,410
and then use planning by using
old concept of control theory,

1246
01:05:05,410 --> 01:05:08,050
such as model predictive
control or whatever,

1247
01:05:08,050 --> 01:05:09,842
to directly convert
that into an agent.

1248
01:05:09,842 --> 01:05:11,550
You don't need
post-training or whatever.

1249
01:05:11,550 --> 01:05:13,100
You can do that zero-shot.

1250
01:05:13,100 --> 01:05:14,700
So that's pretty neat.

1251
01:05:14,700 --> 01:05:19,540
For LLM, I'm not sure, so
I cannot reply for that.

1252
01:05:19,540 --> 01:05:20,640
I think that makes sense.

1253
01:05:20,640 --> 01:05:21,140
Yeah.

1254
01:05:25,300 --> 01:05:28,740
I guess this is--

1255
01:05:28,740 --> 01:05:29,860
let me see.

1256
01:05:29,860 --> 01:05:33,140
Yeah, do you think JEPA models
are less prone to hallucination

1257
01:05:33,140 --> 01:05:36,180
than transformer-based
models, since they hopefully

1258
01:05:36,180 --> 01:05:40,380
learn a more accurate
representation of the world?

1259
01:05:40,380 --> 01:05:42,600
I'm not sure if you guys
might have any insights.

1260
01:05:45,180 --> 01:05:46,640
I can answer first.

1261
01:05:46,640 --> 01:05:51,500
And, Lucas, you can just
add your opinion on it.

1262
01:05:51,500 --> 01:05:56,240
I'm not sure what kind of
hallucination the person asked,

1263
01:05:56,240 --> 01:06:03,580
but in the predictive sense,
because JEPA model is--

1264
01:06:03,580 --> 01:06:06,200
as I mentioned earlier
part of the talk,

1265
01:06:06,200 --> 01:06:08,260
the JEPA is energy-based model.

1266
01:06:08,260 --> 01:06:11,990
So rather than just predicting
the full pixel space, what

1267
01:06:11,990 --> 01:06:18,010
it evaluate is oh, is our
predicted future make sense?

1268
01:06:18,010 --> 01:06:21,590
Is it a possible situation,
or is it impossible?

1269
01:06:21,590 --> 01:06:26,290
So I think in that sense, in
the sense of the representation,

1270
01:06:26,290 --> 01:06:31,550
the representation will be
more suitable for world model,

1271
01:06:31,550 --> 01:06:33,070
I think.

1272
01:06:33,070 --> 01:06:35,938
Yeah.

1273
01:06:35,938 --> 01:06:38,090
Yeah, so I can complete on that.

1274
01:06:38,090 --> 01:06:40,110
So I think the
original question was

1275
01:06:40,110 --> 01:06:42,890
to compare JEPA and
transformer-based model.

1276
01:06:42,890 --> 01:06:44,510
So I would like
just to emphasize

1277
01:06:44,510 --> 01:06:46,870
that JEPA is not
an architecture,

1278
01:06:46,870 --> 01:06:48,550
a new architecture.

1279
01:06:48,550 --> 01:06:50,590
For instance, for
LeWorldModel, [INAUDIBLE]

1280
01:06:50,590 --> 01:06:53,070
encoder and predictor
are two transformers.

1281
01:06:53,070 --> 01:06:57,490
So JEPA is really a framework
to try to learn world model.

1282
01:06:57,490 --> 01:06:58,970
It's not a new architecture.

1283
01:06:58,970 --> 01:07:03,270
And second, I think you can
have definitely a hallucination

1284
01:07:03,270 --> 01:07:04,970
if you don't learn a good model.

1285
01:07:04,970 --> 01:07:08,920
But this is not at all
the same hallucination

1286
01:07:08,920 --> 01:07:14,240
as you have with LLM, because
LLM, they have multiple sources

1287
01:07:14,240 --> 01:07:16,060
from hallucination.

1288
01:07:16,060 --> 01:07:19,320
One of them is that they are not
grounded in the real world, OK?

1289
01:07:19,320 --> 01:07:22,360
With world model, you
can easily fix that.

1290
01:07:22,360 --> 01:07:24,960
Additionally, also, you can
say that with world model,

1291
01:07:24,960 --> 01:07:27,840
you can have another
source of hallucination

1292
01:07:27,840 --> 01:07:30,480
as well is that when
you optimize the action

1293
01:07:30,480 --> 01:07:33,800
sequence that you want
to do for doing planning,

1294
01:07:33,800 --> 01:07:36,320
you could have
actions that are not

1295
01:07:36,320 --> 01:07:38,380
meaningful for the real world.

1296
01:07:38,380 --> 01:07:41,020
For instance, let's say
that your action is minus.

1297
01:07:41,020 --> 01:07:44,380
It's between minus 1 and 1,
and that your optimizer say,

1298
01:07:44,380 --> 01:07:47,560
OK, you should use
minus 2 as an action.

1299
01:07:47,560 --> 01:07:50,120
So you can have this
kind of hallucination.

1300
01:07:50,120 --> 01:07:52,940
Otherwise, it's just about the
quality of your learned model.

1301
01:07:52,940 --> 01:07:56,600
And this is just a matter
of data and capacity.

1302
01:07:56,600 --> 01:07:58,413
Hopefully, that makes sense.

1303
01:07:58,413 --> 01:07:59,080
Yeah, no, great.

1304
01:07:59,080 --> 01:08:00,620
I think that makes
a lot of sense.

1305
01:08:00,620 --> 01:08:02,640
Thanks.

1306
01:08:02,640 --> 01:08:04,520
We have someone asking,
are world models

1307
01:08:04,520 --> 01:08:07,910
better than the diffusion-based
models for robot control?

1308
01:08:12,330 --> 01:08:15,170
I think we would have similar
answer as the previous one.

1309
01:08:15,170 --> 01:08:18,550
The world model can still
use the diffusion model.

1310
01:08:21,850 --> 01:08:23,029
Yeah, of course, of course.

1311
01:08:23,029 --> 01:08:25,050
You could use diffusion
model to train JEPA.

1312
01:08:25,050 --> 01:08:28,609
Actually, some people
try to do that.

1313
01:08:28,609 --> 01:08:32,170
Diffusion model have some
advantage and some limitation

1314
01:08:32,170 --> 01:08:33,450
as well.

1315
01:08:33,450 --> 01:08:35,569
So yeah.

1316
01:08:35,569 --> 01:08:39,250
Any in-person questions?

1317
01:08:39,250 --> 01:08:39,750
All right.

1318
01:08:39,750 --> 01:08:42,410
We got one at the back.

1319
01:08:42,410 --> 01:08:43,630
A longer horizon question.

1320
01:08:43,630 --> 01:08:46,529
But you mentioned that you
might consider planning

1321
01:08:46,529 --> 01:08:50,580
through this whole family of
different algorithms developed

1322
01:08:50,580 --> 01:08:52,830
for control, like model
predictive control, et cetera.

1323
01:08:52,830 --> 01:08:54,410
And you mentioned
that it might be

1324
01:08:54,410 --> 01:08:56,810
possible to use these
things as a grounding signal

1325
01:08:56,810 --> 01:08:57,990
to train policies.

1326
01:08:57,990 --> 01:09:00,330
Do you think that it's
combining those approaches,

1327
01:09:00,330 --> 01:09:02,830
if maybe that's what you already
meant by training policies,

1328
01:09:02,830 --> 01:09:04,529
though, of turning
up a lot of compute

1329
01:09:04,529 --> 01:09:06,569
on something like an alternative
controller for a relatively

1330
01:09:06,569 --> 01:09:08,270
long horizon, and
then distilling those

1331
01:09:08,270 --> 01:09:10,770
back down into policies might
be an efficient way of getting

1332
01:09:10,770 --> 01:09:13,290
some longer horizon behavior
for one of those problems

1333
01:09:13,290 --> 01:09:15,529
and/or research
directions you mentioned?

1334
01:09:19,609 --> 01:09:22,050
You want to go for
it, Hazel, or--

1335
01:09:22,050 --> 01:09:23,490
You can go for it.

1336
01:09:23,490 --> 01:09:25,890
OK.

1337
01:09:25,890 --> 01:09:28,029
Yeah, you can definitely
train a policy

1338
01:09:28,029 --> 01:09:30,529
on that, because as soon as you
have world model, basically,

1339
01:09:30,529 --> 01:09:31,950
you can do either two things.

1340
01:09:31,950 --> 01:09:35,770
You can use that directly and
optimize your action sequence

1341
01:09:35,770 --> 01:09:38,330
to have a zero-shot policy.

1342
01:09:38,330 --> 01:09:40,670
Or you can do the same
as Dreamer, for instance,

1343
01:09:40,670 --> 01:09:43,930
and use your pretrained world
model and train reinforcement

1344
01:09:43,930 --> 01:09:46,649
learning policy on
that, and periodically

1345
01:09:46,649 --> 01:09:49,689
go in your environment,
and collect better data

1346
01:09:49,689 --> 01:09:52,314
and fine-tune your model,
improve your policy,

1347
01:09:52,314 --> 01:09:53,189
and blah, blah, blah.

1348
01:09:53,189 --> 01:09:54,550
You can do that as well.

1349
01:09:54,550 --> 01:09:56,770
So in the future,
I think you will

1350
01:09:56,770 --> 01:10:01,330
have something similar to System
1, System 2, same as human does,

1351
01:10:01,330 --> 01:10:06,820
where you will have basically
an action-reaction policy, where

1352
01:10:06,820 --> 01:10:08,820
you will learn to
directly predict

1353
01:10:08,820 --> 01:10:11,780
what is the action I should
take to have that goal given

1354
01:10:11,780 --> 01:10:12,560
this state.

1355
01:10:12,560 --> 01:10:15,460
And then for difficult
tasks that you are not

1356
01:10:15,460 --> 01:10:18,780
very confident about
planning and model predictive

1357
01:10:18,780 --> 01:10:22,440
control to have more
careful plan and situation.

1358
01:10:22,440 --> 01:10:25,860
For instance, when you want
to learn to drive a car, maybe

1359
01:10:25,860 --> 01:10:28,820
at the first 20 hours, you will
use model predictive control

1360
01:10:28,820 --> 01:10:30,720
to make sure you
don't kill someone.

1361
01:10:30,720 --> 01:10:33,320
And then after 20 hours,
you think you are an expert.

1362
01:10:33,320 --> 01:10:37,500
So you can basically
distill your prediction

1363
01:10:37,500 --> 01:10:42,340
of model predictive
control and planning

1364
01:10:42,340 --> 01:10:45,712
to a direct policy that
outputs directly the action.

1365
01:10:45,712 --> 01:10:46,212
All right.

1366
01:10:46,212 --> 01:10:46,360
Great.

1367
01:10:46,360 --> 01:10:48,152
Unfortunately, that's
all the time we have.

1368
01:10:48,152 --> 01:10:51,740
Hazel will be staying after in
case anybody has any questions

1369
01:10:51,740 --> 01:10:53,540
or wants to talk to her.

1370
01:10:53,540 --> 01:10:57,340
So thanks again to our speakers
for the very insightful talk.

1371
01:10:57,340 --> 01:10:59,170
Thank you.
